-- The arena screen.
--
-- This script owns the frame loop and nothing else about the game. It reads
-- input, calls the simulation core, and draws what the core says is true. No
-- game rule is implemented in Lua, per decision 5: Defold's component update
-- order within a collection is unspecified, and HTML5 builds run Lua 5.1.4
-- rather than LuaJIT, so state distributed across scripts would have neither
-- a defined order nor adequate speed on the platform that matters most.
--
-- Every game is a zone on a server. The client predicts its own ship and
-- draws what arrives; it owns no roster and flies no bots. Before a zone
-- answers there is no world at all, only a starfield with the menu over it.
--
-- Drawing is five mesh layers, built here and in arena/world.lua and
-- arena/ui.lua, and uploaded once each per frame. It used to be
-- `draw_debug3d`, which is one pixel wide, one blend mode, and compiled out
-- of release builds entirely: a shipped bundle drew an empty arena.

local net = require("arena.net")
local frame = require("arena.frame")
local binds = require("arena.binds")
local keys = require("arena.keys")
local touch = require("arena.touch")
local account = require("arena.account")
local directory = require("arena.directory")
local menu = require("arena.menu")
local pal = require("arena.palette")
local marks = require("arena.marks")
local prize = require("arena.prize")
local world = require("arena.world")
local fx = require("arena.fx")
local sfx = require("arena.sfx")
local ui = require("arena.ui")
local vec = require("render.vec")

-- Every tuned number in this file, on one table rather than on fifteen locals.
--
-- LuaJIT allows a function sixty upvalues, and `update` is one frame of a game:
-- it reaches the modules above, the state below, and a constant wherever a
-- constant is wanted. Counted individually those came to sixty-six and the
-- bundle stopped compiling, with an error naming a line nine hundred lines from
-- anything to do with the cause. A table is one upvalue however much it holds,
-- so the ceiling stops being something a new constant can walk into.
--
-- Each number is still declared where it is explained, beside the code that
-- reads it. This is only the namespace they share.
local TUNE = {}
TUNE.CONTROL = require("arena.control_actions").new(hash, sim)

TUNE.TICK_HZ = 100
TUNE.DT = 1 / TUNE.TICK_HZ
TUNE.MAX_CATCHUP = 8      -- ticks per frame; a long stall must not stampede
TUNE.DETAIL_R2 = world.DETAIL_RANGE * world.DETAIL_RANGE
-- Where a hull starts reading as damaged, as a fraction of full energy.
-- The same threshold your own pip already grades at, so the outline and
-- the bar do not disagree about when you are in trouble.
TUNE.HURT_AT = 0.45

TUNE.CLASS_NAMES = {"Apex", "Wedge", "Chord", "Anvil",
                     "Cipher", "Facet", "Lattice"}

local held = {}
-- A tap can go down and up inside one frame, so the frame loop never sees it
-- in `held`. Presses are latched here and consumed by whatever reads them,
-- which is how the menu gets a keystroke that flight does not need.
local tapped = {}
-- What each key press turned into, so its release takes the same thing down.
-- Not cleared every frame the way `tapped` is: a key is held across frames and
-- this is the record of what it is holding.
local owner = {}
-- The keys that came up this frame, cleared beside `tapped`. One caller: the
-- controls page, which knows a chord is finished when the first of its keys
-- is let go.
local lifted = {}
-- What the emulated pointer already pressed this frame, so the touch behind it
-- does not press the same control a second time.
--
-- Defold raises a mouse action for every touch as well as the touch itself,
-- and `touch.used` is what keeps that echo out of the interface. It cannot
-- keep out the first one. Both halves of a tap arrive in the same frame, the
-- mouse half is handed over first, and on the first tap of a session nothing
-- has reported a touch yet: the echo goes through the hit boxes, and the touch
-- behind it presses the same control again. On a stop that is a panel opened
-- and shut inside one frame, so the button lights and nothing opens, and the
-- tap after it works because `touch.used` is set by then. Once a session, on
-- the first thing anybody presses.
--
-- Cleared beside `tapped`: it is a fact about one frame.
local pointer_took = nil
local me = 0
-- Watching rather than flying. While this is set, `me` is 255 and must never
-- reach a `sim.*` accessor: the extension refuses an index that wide, loudly,
-- which is the backstop under every branch below that goes through `eye()`
-- instead.
local watching = false
-- What the clock last said out loud, so a message that repeats says nothing
-- twice. Either clock: the one counting down to a match and the one counting
-- down inside it both spend their last five seconds out loud. The rule is
-- `net.clock_says`; this is the half of its state that fits up here.
local counted_at = nil

-- The ship whose perspective drives the camera, the colors and the sounds:
-- your own hull, or the snapshot's subject while watching. Nil when there is
-- nobody to stand behind, which is an empty room's channel.
local function eye()
    if not watching then return me end
    local s = net.subject
    if s and s ~= 255 and s < sim.ship_count() then return s end
    return nil
end

-- The side everything on screen is colored against.
--
-- Your own while flying. Also your own while watching, which is the side you
-- sat out from and the zone tells a watcher what it is: coloring from the
-- subject's side instead was the obvious reading and the wrong one, because
-- the camera moves. It would have repainted your own teammates as enemies the
-- moment the channel picked somebody from the other side.
--
-- 255 belongs to no side, which is what a stranger who arrived to watch gets:
-- nobody out there is theirs, and nothing reads as friendly.
local function eye_team()
    if watching then return net.my_team end
    local e = eye()
    return e and sim.ship_team(e) or 255
end

-- What the interface should say this connection is looking at, or nil while
-- flying: the hull the room's camera stands behind, which is nobody this
-- client picked and nobody it can pick.
local function watch_state()
    if not watching then return nil end
    return {subject = eye()}
end
local accum = 0
local feed = {}
local hurt = 0
-- Ticks this pilot has spent inside a safe zone without leaving it, which is
-- the same count the room is keeping and for the same reason: it is about to
-- take the seat back. Counted here rather than read off the wire because it
-- is a number the client can work out from the hull it already predicts, and
-- a byte a snapshot would have to carry twenty times a second to say the same
-- thing. The two clocks run at the same rate from the same event and the
-- number is read in seconds, so a tick of drift is not a thing anybody sees.
local safe_ticks = 0
-- Whether the fan was declined last frame, so the frame after a press can
-- hear that it landed. Nil until a hull has been read once: a seat this
-- client has not looked at yet has no previous state to have changed from.
local multi_was = nil
-- The last loadout this pilot actually flew: levels, add-ons, the fan's
-- switch and what was in hand, refreshed every living frame and drawn while
-- the hull is gone. Death strips a ship in one instruction, so without this
-- the pads and the corner stack drop to plain green rounds and empty racks
-- the moment you die and stay that way for the whole respawn wait, which is
-- the interface rearranging itself over the card that says what killed you.
--
-- One table, filled in place rather than built per frame: this runs on every
-- frame of every game and a fresh table sixty times a second is garbage the
-- collector has to walk in the middle of a fight.
local flown = {level = {}, mods = {}, charge = {}, multi_off = false, cls = 0}
local flown_ok = false
-- This frame's answer, set once in update before anything draws: charge_row
-- and the pads both read it, so the two cannot disagree about what is held.
local held_kit = nil
-- What the interface should draw from this frame: nil while the hull is
-- there to be asked, the copy while it is not.
local function loadout_held()
    if sim.ship_alive(me) == 1 then
        for t = 0, sim.TRIG_COUNT - 1 do
            flown.level[t] = sim.ship_level(me, t)
            local m = flown.mods[t]
            if not m then m = {} flown.mods[t] = m end
            for i = 0, sim.MOD_COUNT - 1 do m[i] = sim.ship_mod(me, t, i) end
        end
        for k = 0, sim.MAX_CHARGES - 1 do
            flown.charge[k] = sim.ship_charge(me, k)
        end
        flown.multi_off = sim.ship_multi_off(me)
        -- The hull, because the volley a trigger throws is read off the
        -- pattern its class flies at that rung: without it a mark drawn
        -- through the respawn wait asks the core about hull zero.
        flown.cls = sim.ship_class(me)
        flown_ok = true
        return nil
    end
    return flown_ok and flown or nil
end
-- Which zone the "nobody is running X" note is about, so the note can be
-- withdrawn when the games list shows that zone live again.
local note_zone = nil
-- Per-seat bank state, described where it is used further down. Declared up
-- here because go_home clears it, and a local declared below the function that
-- reads it is not the same variable: the function closes over a global that is
-- nil, and clearing it throws on the way out of every zone.
local rolls = {}
local layers = nil
-- The terrain window, in tiles either side of the camera, and how far the
-- camera may walk before it is rebuilt. A map is 1024 tiles square, so what
-- gets meshed is a window rather than the map.
--
-- It follows the view, and has to. The camera holds a fixed zoom per decision
-- 13, so a wider window sees further rather than seeing the same thing
-- bigger: half the window in tiles is the drawable's half-width over sixteen.
-- This was 56, a constant, written against "a view about 20 tiles wide" and
-- correct for exactly one size of browser. At 1280 CSS pixels the view is 40
-- tiles either side and 56 leaves precisely one rebuild's margin; past that
-- the margin goes negative and terrain stops in mid air, short of an edge the
-- player can plainly see over. Rebuilding does not close it, because the
-- window is recentered rather than widened, which is why it reads as a gap
-- that follows you.
TUNE.STATIC_STEP = 16
-- What a 1280-wide window needs, and the floor: a window smaller than this
-- gains nothing from meshing less, and the home screen has no viewport yet.
TUNE.STATIC_MIN = 56
-- A ceiling on one rebuild, in tiles either side. The window is meshed whole
-- and held whole, so its cost is the square of this: 137 is a 3840-wide
-- drawable with its rebuild margin intact, and asks about 22 MB of the two
-- static layers. Past it the old gap comes back, far out, which is the trade
-- against a client that would otherwise ask for hundreds of megabytes on a
-- wall display. Note this is CSS pixels rather than device ones: a dense
-- display folds its density into the zoom, so a retina laptop sees the same
-- amount of world as a plain one and asks for the same window.
TUNE.STATIC_MAX = 137
local static_half = TUNE.STATIC_MIN
-- What a static layer starts at before any build has said what it needs, and
-- the floor it is never taken below. The window is meshed rarely and drawn
-- always, so what a capacity really costs is a per-frame one: the mesh
-- component submits the whole buffer every frame, degenerate or not, and a
-- capacity chosen for the densest tile of the map is paid for over the empty
-- ones too. So these are a starting guess and `fit_static` below moves them.
TUNE.STATIC_SEED = 16384
TUNE.STATIC_FLOOR = 8192
-- Where the static field was last rebuilt. A table for the reason TUNE is
-- one: a pair of bare locals is a pair of upvalues in `update`.
local static = {tx = nil, ty = nil}
-- Forward declared: leaving a zone is reached both from a menu row and from a
-- socket that dropped, and one of those runs above the place that knows what
-- a menu action means.
local apply_menu
local go_home
-- And the line that says on the ship panel's head where the draft it is
-- holding gets spent. The frame loop draws it and it runs above the gate it
-- reads.
local ship_foot
-- And the menu's own presses, which both hands reach: the pointer path is
-- written where every other press is, a long way below the frame loop that
-- reads the keyboard.
local menu_act

-- HTML5 builds run Lua 5.1, which has no bitwise operators. Every bitfield in
-- this client is a sum of distinct powers of two, so a digit test is exact.
local function has(bits, bit)
    return math.floor(bits / bit) % 2 == 1
end

-- Defold hands on_input a hashed action_id, never the string it was written
-- as in the input binding. Comparing it to a string literal silently yields
-- false for every key ever pressed, which is exactly what it did.
local function bit_for(action)
    return TUNE.CONTROL.bits[action] or TUNE.CONTROL.pointer_bits[action] or 0
end

-- Whether the page holds the keyboard lock, polled from the fullscreen state
-- once a second. While it is held, Ctrl fires guns and the layout is the
-- original's outright. While it is not, Ctrl does nothing at all, which is
-- the honest reading of a key the browser owns half of: windowed, Ctrl with
-- Tab switches tabs on every platform and no preventDefault is consulted, so
-- a Ctrl that fired would be a trap sprung by the game's commonest chord.
local kb_locked = false

-- The charge slots this hull can ever carry, in order. An empty list means a
-- hull with no charges at all, and the row simply is not drawn.
-- Every room of the game we are in, as the directory last described it.
--
-- From the directory rather than from the arena we are connected to. That
-- process knows its own rooms and nothing about the ones its siblings hold,
-- and this list is the whole zone's, so the only thing that can answer is the
-- service that hears from all of them. Nil until the directory has said, which
-- is also the answer for a zone holding one room: the corner chip and the
-- panel are both drawn from this, so neither appears until there is a choice
-- to make.
local function my_rooms()
    if menu.zone == "" then return nil end
    for _, r in ipairs(directory.rows) do
        if r.zone == menu.zone then return r.rooms end
    end
    return nil
end

-- The zone's sides, by the byte the simulation knows each as, for anything
-- that wants to print one. The roster carries them already; this is the same
-- list keyed the way a caller with a team byte in hand can use.
local function side_names()
    local out = {}
    for _, t in ipairs(net.teams) do
        if t.public then out[t.team] = t.name end
    end
    return out
end

-- The charge kinds this pilot is actually carrying, in kind order, which is
-- the order Q and W spend them in.
--
-- Off the hull's own profile rather than off a zone-wide ceiling. A ceiling
-- is what a rack may hold and every hull in the zone shares it, so it named
-- every kind for everybody and bound keys to charges the pilot was not
-- carrying. A hull carries two kinds and which two is the hull's, so the
-- profile is the only thing that can say which key spends what.
--
-- The profile, not the count in hand: the corner shows what is left, and
-- binding to that would move Q onto the burst the moment the last repel went,
-- which is a key that spends the wrong thing exactly when a fight is going
-- badly.
local function my_slots()
    local out = {}
    if watching then return out end
    for k = 0, sim.MAX_CHARGES - 1 do
        if sim.ship_kit(me, sim.SLOT_CHARGE0 + k) > 0 then
            out[#out + 1] = k
        end
    end
    -- Which of the two the first key throws is the pilot's to decide, and the
    -- ship page is where they decide it. Read here rather than sent: the kit
    -- carries how many of each kind, and which key spends which is a fact
    -- about this keyboard. See `menu.swap_charges`.
    if menu.charge_flip and #out > 1 then out[1], out[2] = out[2], out[1] end
    return out
end

-- The charge a pad asked for this frame, read from the latch once and held
-- until the step has used it.
--
-- It cannot be consumed inside `my_buttons`: that runs more than once a frame,
-- for the network, for the simulation and to know whether to draw an engine
-- flame, so the first call would swallow the tap and the one that mattered
-- would see nothing.
local charge_tap = nil
-- Declared here and defined down with the menu acts, because update() above
-- the definition rejoins with it: a local is resolved at compile time, and a
-- function written above the `local` it uses reads a nil global instead. That
-- exact mistake is the reason the lint job exists.
-- Joining a game, and the loop that keeps this client in one: one table,
-- because update() reaches sixty enclosing locals and LuaJIT stops counting
-- there. `session.enter` is the join a press asks for; `session.seek_tick`
-- is what dials a room when there is none, which is what opening the client
-- does.
local session = {
    browser = require("arena.browser"),
    deep_route = require("arena.deep_route"),
    playback = require("arena.playback"),
}

session.replay_route = session.deep_route.new({
    ready = function() return account.base ~= "" end,
    note = function(text) menu.note = text end,
    fetch = function(id, callback) account.fetch_replay(id, callback) end,
    start = function(state, reply) session.replay(state, reply) end,
})

local function uri_read(s)
    return (s or ""):gsub("%%(%x%x)", function(h)
        return string.char(tonumber(h, 16))
    end)
end

function session.enter_deep_route(self)
    local route = self.deep_route
    if type(route) ~= "string" or route == "" then return false end
    local replay = session.replay_route(self, route)
    if replay ~= nil then return replay end
    local kind, zone, instance, room = string.match(route,
        "^(join)/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/(%d+)$")
    if not kind then
        kind, zone, instance, room = string.match(route,
            "^(watch)/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/(%d+)$")
    end
    if not kind then
        kind, zone = string.match(route, "^(join)/([^/]+)$")
        if not kind then kind, zone = string.match(route, "^(watch)/([^/]+)$") end
        if not kind then
            self.deep_route = nil
            return false
        end
        zone = uri_read(zone)
        local row = nil
        for _, candidate in ipairs(directory.rows) do
            if candidate.zone == zone and candidate.live then row = candidate end
        end
        if not row then return false end
        self.deep_route = nil
        menu.zone = zone
        menu.save_identity()
        menu.close()
        return session.enter(self, row.address, zone, row.wt, nil,
                             kind == "watch", row.instance)
    end
    zone, instance, room = uri_read(zone), uri_read(instance), tonumber(room)
    local at = directory.at_instance(instance)
    if not at or at.zone ~= zone then return false end
    self.deep_route = nil
    menu.zone = zone
    menu.save_identity()
    menu.close()
    return session.enter(self, at.address, zone, at.wt, room,
                         kind == "watch", instance)
end
-- A latch for a charge key, filled where taps are read and drained
-- into `charge_tap` by the step loop.
local key_charge = nil

local function my_buttons()
    if menu.open then return 0 end
    -- A watcher has no ship to press anything into.
    if watching then return 0 end
    -- Collected as a set of distinct bits and summed, rather than or-ed:
    -- HTML5 builds run Lua 5.1, which has no bitwise operators, and a bit
    -- contributed by both the keyboard and the stick must still count once.
    local bits = {}
    local steering = touch.steering()
    for action, down in pairs(held) do
        -- Defold raises a mouse action for every touch as well as the touch
        -- itself. On a phone that made the whole screen a trigger: putting a
        -- thumb down to steer fired the guns, and there was no way to turn
        -- without shooting. Once a device has shown it has a touchscreen the
        -- emulated pointer is ignored and the pads are the only triggers.
        local emulated = touch.used and (action == hash("pointer")
                                         or action == hash("pointer_alt"))
        -- Ctrl is a gun only while the keyboard lock holds. Gated here rather
        -- than at the binding, because the binding cannot know and a Ctrl that
        -- spent a charge or fired unlocked would be worse than one that does
        -- nothing.
        local unlocked_ctrl = action == hash("guns_ctrl") and not kb_locked
        if down and not emulated and not unlocked_ctrl then
            -- Two sources on the rudder would fight; the stick wins while it
            -- is down, and the keys keep everything else.
            local bit = bit_for(action)
            local rudder = bit == sim.BTN_LEFT or bit == sim.BTN_RIGHT
            if not (steering and rudder) then bits[bit] = true end
        end
    end
    -- Raw: the stick compares the swipe against where the nose actually is,
    -- and a heading eased for the screen is not a fact about the ship.
    for _, bit in ipairs(touch.bits(sim.ship_heading_raw(me))) do
        bits[bit] = true
    end
    -- A spend names its own slot, whichever control asked: a pad's tap and a
    -- key's press land in the same latch, so there is no selection anywhere
    -- to read back or to have forgotten to move. The control you pressed is
    -- the thing you get.
    if charge_tap then bits[sim.BTN_USE] = true end
    -- Multifire is the keyboard's alone. It was an upward pull off the gun pad
    -- for a while, which is a gesture nobody finds on a control they are
    -- holding down, and the pad's mark already draws the volley a pull throws,
    -- so what the gesture bought was a state nobody could reach and nobody had
    -- asked for. The key still holds sim.BTN_MULTI through `held` above.

    local b = 0
    for bit in pairs(bits) do b = b + bit end
    -- Which charge is spent rides in the buttons rather than in the
    -- simulation: selection is a thing a pilot does with their own interface,
    -- so it costs no snapshot byte and no edge detection down in the core.
    return b + (charge_tap or 0) * sim.BTN_SLOT_STEP
end

-- What the panel draws: every slot this hull can carry, how many are in
-- hand, and which one the use key would spend.
local function charge_row()
    local out = {}
    if watching then return out end
    for _, k in ipairs(my_slots()) do
        local c = pal.CHARGES[k + 1]
        out[#out + 1] = {
            short = c and c.short or "C",
            -- The full word, because the corner stack has room for it and
            -- "RPL" is three letters nobody has been taught.
            name = c and c.name or "charge",
            count = held_kit and held_kit.charge[k] or sim.ship_charge(me, k),
            -- How deep this hull's rack is, so the row draws the empties as
            -- well as the full ones. The hull's own number rather than a
            -- zone-wide ceiling: a ceiling is the same fifteen for everybody,
            -- so the rail drew a pilot carrying three repels as three of
            -- fifteen.
            max = sim.ship_kit(me, sim.SLOT_CHARGE0 + k),
            -- How long until this kind may be thrown again, and how long it
            -- waits when it is. A burst shuts its own key behind it and the
            -- corner has to say so, or a pilot presses a key that does
            -- nothing and learns nothing from it.
            --
            -- Off the hull rather than the copy above, because a death does
            -- not clear this clock: the rack is not re-dealt at a respawn, so
            -- neither is the wait in front of it.
            wait = sim.ship_charge_wait(me, k),
            delay = sim.charge_delay(k),
        }
    end
    return out
end

-- How far up the screen the thumb's controls reach, in drawable pixels from
-- the bottom: the top of the gun pad, or of the charge rail when one is
-- carried. The interface places its own things clear of this rather than
-- guessing at a fraction of the screen.
local function pad_reach(w, h, s)
    local L = touch.layout(w, h, s)
    local reach = L.guns.y + L.guns.r
    -- Every satellite, not only the charges: the bomb rides the same orbit
    -- now, so on a hull carrying no charge at all the rack is what the hand
    -- reaches highest with.
    for _, c in ipairs(L.sats or {}) do
        local top = c.y + c.r
        if top > reach then reach = top end
    end
    return reach
end

-- The menu and the info panels are the same kind of thing, a slab of text
-- laid over a fight that does not pause for it, so they are one at a time.
-- Opening either shuts the other. Two of them at once was the exact thing the
-- HUD pass existed to stop, arrived at from a different direction.
-- One slab of text at a time, and the rule holds in both directions or it
-- holds in neither. The left column draws one panel: the rooms list stands in
-- that slot and wins it. The menu is the same argument a layer up, and its hit
-- boxes are published after the panel's, so a panel left open under it
-- swallows every press that lands in its rectangle.
--
-- The players sheet is not in this list any more, because it is not a panel
-- of its own: it is a stop of the menu, so opening the menu is what opens it
-- and this closes it by closing the menu.
local function shut_panels()
    -- The controls table belongs in this list for the same reason the
    -- scoreboard does: the toggles below treat the two as one slot, and help
    -- was the half that opening the menu forgot. It is not drawn under an open
    -- menu, so the symptom was a table nobody asked for reappearing when the
    -- menu closed, including over a game joined in the meantime.
    ui.help = false
    ui.rooms_open = false
    ui.room_ask = nil
    -- And the level the ship stop was open at, with the cursor: the column
    -- comes up on its bare stops every time.
    ui.col_sect, ui.col_hull = nil, nil
    ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
    ui.page_scroll = 0
    -- And the slide with it, rather than left half up for the next panel to
    -- inherit: this is the whole screen changing hands, not a panel closing.
    ui.panel_shut()
end

-- A credit spent, or a refusal.
--
-- The two answers a step of the ship menu has, said out loud. Nothing was said
-- for the second one: a pilot with an empty purse pressed the up arrow on a
-- charge and the client did nothing at all, which reads as a control that has
-- stopped working rather than as a purse with nothing in it. The interface
-- makes a noise for everything else it is asked, so silence here is the odd
-- one out.
--
-- One sound for every way a step can be refused, because they are one thing
-- to a hand: the credit is not there, the slot is already at its ceiling, or
-- the hull cannot reach it at all.
local function spend(moved)
    sfx.ui(moved and "ui_go" or "ui_deny")
end

-- Where the cursor stands after the column has changed level: on the head of
-- whatever page is open, and on the stop itself once the last page has come
-- off it.
--
-- Nothing stood on either at first. The stop a panel climbs off goes out
-- through the bottom edge with the column, so the cursor was left on a control
-- that is no longer drawn: the panel opened with none of its rows lit, a hand
-- on the arrows had to press one to find the top of a page it was already
-- looking at, and a hand on a pointer had nothing saying which level of the
-- stack it was reading. This was two functions while the menu was two menus.
--
-- `stop` is the stop that level hangs off, which only matters where there is
-- no page left to stand on: the stack has already been popped by then and
-- cannot say which stop it was.
local function menu_cursor(stop)
    if #menu.stack > 0 then
        ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = "menu_back", nil
    else
        ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = "menu_stop", stop
    end
end

-- One level out of the ship panel, where that is where the way back leads.
--
-- The ship stop's levels are the panel's rather than the stack's: the menu
-- and the part of the ship open over it are one entry on the stack between
-- them, so stepping out of a part is a thing only this knows how to do.
-- Both keys that mean "back" ask it first, or escape would leave the stop and
-- the part in one press while the head's own arrow took two.
--
-- What the cursor is left standing on is the part it came out of, which is
-- what took the panel's place on the screen.
-- Out of a pilot's card onto the players sheet, which is one level like any
-- other: the card is a panel that stacked on the sheet, so back steps off it
-- before it steps out of the stop. The cursor lands on the row it opened
-- from, which is where it stood when the card came up.
local function board_card_back()
    if menu.stop_open() ~= "players" or not ui.col_pilot then return false end
    ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = "board_row", ui.board_row_of(ui.col_pilot)
    ui.col_pilot = nil
    ui.page_scroll = 0
    return true
end

local function ship_sect_back()
    if menu.stop_open() ~= "ship" or not ui.col_sect then return false end
    ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = "land_sect", ui.col_sect
    ui.col_sect, ui.col_hull = nil, nil
    ui.col_scroll = 0
    return true
end

local function toggle_menu()
    if menu.toggle() then shut_panels() end
end

-- The players sheet, raised at its own stop.
--
-- It was a board with its own key, its own column and its own panel; it is a
-- stop of the menu now, so what the key does is open the menu already standing
-- on it. Everything else about it follows from that: escape walks out of it
-- the way it walks out of any panel, the arrows walk its rows, and a press
-- opens the card the way a press opens any row. See decision 147.
--
-- Not while there is no room: the sheet is a list of who is in one.
local function open_players()
    if menu.adrift then return false end
    shut_panels()
    menu.open = true
    menu.stack = {"players"}
    -- On the list rather than on a card left open from last time, and at the
    -- top of it: a stop opens where the room is rather than where the last
    -- reader left off.
    ui.col_pilot = nil
    ui.page_scroll = 0
    menu_cursor("players")
    return true
end

local function toggle_players()
    if menu.open and menu.stop_open() == "players" then
        menu.close()
        ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
        return
    end
    open_players()
end

-- The controls table. A slab of text like the scoreboard, so it shuts the
-- same things the scoreboard shuts: two of those over each other is neither.
local function toggle_help()
    if ui.help_prompt then
        ui.help_prompt = false
        menu.dismiss_help_prompt()
    end
    ui.help = not ui.help
    if ui.help then
        menu.close()
        ui.rooms_open = false
        ui.room_ask = nil
    end
end

-- The map is not one of those. It is an instrument rather than a slab of
-- text: it stands in the radar's corner, it covers no more of the fight than
-- the radar already did, and reading it while the scoreboard is up is a
-- reasonable thing to want. So it toggles on its own and shuts nothing.
local function toggle_map()
    ui.map = not ui.map
end

-- Panel input is data-driven so the binding catalog and the frame loop share
-- one executable contract. The handlers keep the old ordering and gates: a
-- panel press is consumed even without a live arena, while Escape remains for
-- the open menu to handle itself.
TUNE.PANEL_HANDLERS = {
    menu = function()
        -- A card is the innermost panel of all, and it answers escape itself:
        -- `menu.step` gives the last of its keys, the one that changes
        -- nothing. Handled where the rest of that card's keys are handled
        -- rather than here, so there is one way out of a card and not two.
        if menu.ask then return false end
        -- With the column up, escape walks back out of it: a page first, then
        -- the column itself. One press put it up and one press takes it down
        -- from its own bare stops, which is what the key has always meant;
        -- what it does not do any more is close a page and the panel around it
        -- in the same press.
        --
        -- At home the last of those steps is refused rather than handled here:
        -- `menu.close` comes back to the bare stops and leaves the column
        -- standing, because out there there is nothing behind it to close on
        -- to. So escape on the front page walks out of whatever is open and
        -- then does nothing, which is what it has always done out there.
        if menu.open then
            local stop = menu.stack[1]
            if ship_sect_back() then
                sfx.ui("ui_move")
                return true
            end
            if menu.page_back() then
                ui.page_scroll = 0
                menu_cursor(stop)
            else
                menu.close()
                ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
            end
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
            return true
        end
        -- Escape dismisses the innermost open panel before opening the menu.
        if ui.room_ask then
            ui.room_ask = nil
        elseif ui.help then
            ui.help = false
        elseif ui.rooms_open then
            ui.rooms_open = false
        else
            toggle_menu()
            sfx.ui("ui_go")
            return true
        end
        sfx.ui("ui_move")
        return true
    end,
    details = function(_, live)
        if live then
            toggle_players()
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
        end
    end,
    map = function(_, live)
        if live then
            toggle_map()
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
        end
    end,
    help = function(_, live)
        -- Only where there is a room, like the scoreboard beside it. The
        -- controls table is a slab of text over a fight and the menu is the
        -- other one, and the two are one at a time: a table raised with
        -- nothing behind it would be drawn over the loader's own picture.
        -- The controls are a page of the settings stop, which is one press
        -- away wherever you are standing.
        if live and not menu.typing() then
            toggle_help()
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
        end
    end,
}

-- What the pointer is resting on in the menu, which lights the same way the
-- arrow keys light a row: one cursor, moved by either hand.
--
-- Asked every frame rather than on the mouse event, because the thing under
-- the pointer moves on its own: the games list refills every three seconds
-- and a long one scrolls under the cursor. A highlight that only followed the
-- mouse would sit on whatever slid beneath it. It is a walk of a dozen
-- rectangles and it costs nothing.
--
-- Reports whether the pointer arrived somewhere, so the caller can make the
-- noise a key makes. Leaving a row is silent: there is nothing to say.


-- The same pass again, for the column. Its stops, its key and an open list
-- are the menu: they are everything a first visit presses, and they were the
-- only controls on the client that a pointer could not light.
--
-- `ui.pick` for the one copy of
-- the rule, so a row of an open list beats the stop behind it and the backdrop
-- that swallows a press outside the list lights nothing; every frame rather
-- than on the mouse event, since the zone list is refilled off the directory
-- for as long as it is open; and the answer is whether the pointer arrived
-- somewhere, so the caller can make the noise a key makes.
--
-- The cursor it writes is also the arrows', so what it compares against is the
-- pointer's own last answer rather than the cursor itself. A hand lying still moves nothing,
-- which is what lets the arrows walk away from a pointer parked on a stop,
-- and a hand that leaves a control and comes back makes the noise again.
-- Every row a panel publishes, because every row a panel publishes lights.
--
-- This was a list of the controls that happened to have been thought about,
-- and two whole panels were missing from it: an account act and a hull's slot
-- lit under the arrows and stayed dark under a pointer resting on them. One
-- row, one way of being under a hand, whichever hand it is.
local LAND_HOT = {
    menu_go = true, menu_stop = true, menu_pick = true, menu_row = true,
    menu_back = true,
    -- And the ship panel's own rows, which are the one stop with a second
    -- level inside it.
    land_pick_ship = true, land_kit_row = true, land_kit_reset = true,
    land_sect = true, land_flair = true, land_page_ship = true,
}

-- The arrows either side of a value, and the row each pair belongs to.
--
-- An arrow is not a control of its own: it steps the row it stands on, and
-- left and right on that row do the same thing without it. So a hand resting
-- on one stands on the row, which is what the drawing already assumes. It did
-- not: an arrow publishes its own box at a higher priority than the row's, and
-- none of these actions is in `LAND_HOT`, so a pointer moving onto one took
-- the cursor off the row it was about to spend on and left the panel with
-- nothing lit. Scrubbing the body carousel was the loud case, since the whole
-- of choosing a hull is those two arrows.
--
-- The second field is where the row's own value sits in the table an arrow
-- carries, or nothing where the row has no value to tell it apart by.
local LAND_ARROW = {
    land_page_ship = {"land_pick_ship"},
    land_kit_step = {"land_kit_row", "slot"},
    land_flair_step = {"land_flair", "index"},
    menu_step = {"menu_row", "index"},
}

-- What the pointer last answered, and where it was standing when it answered.
-- One table rather than four locals beside each other: this file is a chunk of
-- module handles and session state with a frame function reaching most of them,
-- and a table is one upvalue however many fields it holds. See
-- `upvalues_test.lua`.
local land_over = {}
local function land_hover(x, y, vh)
    -- A hand lying still moves nothing. That has to hold when the screen moves
    -- under the hand as well as when it does not, and it did not: the boxes
    -- were read again on every frame, so a stop opening a panel handed the
    -- cursor to whatever that panel had drawn under a pointer nobody was
    -- touching, and the head a panel opens on lost it the frame after it
    -- landed there. Ask the pointer where it is once it has gone somewhere.
    if not land_over.stale and x == land_over.x and y == land_over.y
       and vh == land_over.h and touch.used == land_over.touched
    then
        return false
    end
    land_over.stale = nil
    land_over.x, land_over.y = x, y
    land_over.h, land_over.touched = vh, touch.used
    local act, val = nil, nil
    if x and not touch.used then
        local r = ui.pick(x, vh - y, false)
        local arrow = r and LAND_ARROW[r.action]
        if arrow then
            act = arrow[1]
            if arrow[2] and type(r.value) == "table" then
                val = r.value[arrow[2]]
            end
        elseif r and LAND_HOT[r.action] then
            act, val = r.action, r.value
        end
    end
    if act == land_over.action and val == land_over.value then return false end
    land_over.action, land_over.value = act, val
    -- The pointer takes the cursor on arrival and gives it up on the way out.
    ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = act, val
    return act ~= nil
end

-- Ask it again anyway, once, with the hand where it is.
--
-- The wheel is the one thing that puts a different control under a still hand
-- without being a press this file has already answered: the rows slide and the
-- pointer does not, so a hand resting on the settings page would otherwise go
-- on lighting the row it started over until it moved.
local function land_slid()
    land_over.stale = true
end

-- The terrain and the starfield never move, so they are built once per map
-- rather than per frame. Rebuilding them was the single most expensive thing
-- the old renderer did, and it did it sixty times a second.
-- What the two static layers should hold next time, decided by what they held
-- last time. Applied before the next build rather than after this one, because
-- resizing hands a layer an empty buffer and rebuilding twice to fill it would
-- put the whole cost of the change into one frame.
local bg_want, bgglow_want = nil, nil

-- Choose a capacity from what a build actually used.
--
-- Density varies across a map far more than the window size does: a window of
-- open space costs almost nothing to mesh and the middle of Chaos costs
-- everything. Sizing for the worst tile everywhere is what made the layers
-- expensive, since the whole buffer is drawn every frame whether it holds
-- geometry or not.
--
-- Half again as much as the last build is the target. It is enough that
-- ordinary drift across a map does not touch the capacity at all, and the
-- band below is wide enough that a layer does not thrash between two sizes on
-- a ship flying back and forth across one wall.
local function fit_static(layer, dropped)
    -- The extension reports vertices written and shapes refused. A refused
    -- static shape can be a six-vertex quad, so count the worst case here. The
    -- retry is then large enough for the exact build that overflowed it.
    local used = layer.n + dropped * 6
    local want = math.max(TUNE.STATIC_FLOOR, math.ceil(used * 1.5))
    if used > layer.cap * 0.9 or want < layer.cap * 0.55 then
        return want
    end
    return nil
end

local function rebuild_static(tx, ty)
    if not layers then return end
    if not tx then
        -- Whatever is on screen, or the middle of the map before anything is.
        local e = eye()
        if e then
            tx = math.floor(sim.ship_x(e) / 16)
            ty = math.floor(sim.ship_y(e) / 16)
        else
            tx, ty = 512, 512
        end
    end
    static.tx, static.ty = tx, ty
    -- Spawns and goals are drawn in the color of whoever owns them, and the
    -- terrain is built long before the frame that draws a ship, so the team
    -- goes in here rather than being passed down every call.
    world.my_team = (sim.ship_count() > 0) and eye_team() or 0
    -- Whatever the last build asked for, before this one writes anything.
    if bg_want then layers.bg:resize(bg_want); bg_want = nil end
    if bgglow_want then layers.bgglow:resize(bgglow_want); bgglow_want = nil end
    world.build_static(layers.bg, layers.bgglow,
                       tx - static_half, ty - static_half,
                       tx + static_half, ty + static_half)
    -- What this one turned out to need. A layer that dropped geometry is
    -- short right now rather than next time, so it is rebuilt at once; that
    -- is the one case worth paying two builds for, because the alternative is
    -- terrain missing from the screen until the camera happens to move.
    bg_want = fit_static(layers.bg, layers.bg.dropped)
    bgglow_want = fit_static(layers.bgglow, layers.bgglow.dropped)
    if layers.bg.dropped > 0 or layers.bgglow.dropped > 0 then
        if bg_want then layers.bg:resize(bg_want); bg_want = nil end
        if bgglow_want then layers.bgglow:resize(bgglow_want); bgglow_want = nil end
        world.build_static(layers.bg, layers.bgglow,
                           tx - static_half, ty - static_half,
                           tx + static_half, ty + static_half)
    end
end

-- No terrain at all, which is what the home screen wants. The static layers
-- hold whatever map was last built, and a menu over the walls of the zone you
-- just left reads as a game still running behind it.
local function clear_static()
    world.forget_overview()
    if not layers then return end
    layers.bg:reset()
    layers.bg:flush()
    layers.bgglow:reset()
    layers.bgglow:flush()
    static.tx, static.ty = nil, nil
end

-- Back to the menu with nothing behind it.
--
-- Every way out of a zone lands here: the leave row, a socket that dropped, a
-- join that was never answered, and the first frame of the whole client. The
-- note is deliberately not cleared, because most of the callers have just set
-- one and it is the only thing that will say what went wrong.
-- Put down the zone we were in.
--
-- Both ways out of a game run this: going back to the menu, and joining
-- another one. They used to hold two copies of the list, and the copies had
-- already parted -- the box open on a pilot and the switch positions the pads
-- were drawing were cleared by one and not the other, so a zone-to-zone join
-- carried a panel describing a seat the next room deals to a stranger. What
-- each caller does *differently* is worth reading; what they do the same is
-- not worth writing twice.
--
-- The rooms panel is deliberately not here. See session.enter.
local function leave_world()
    -- `net.connect` drops the old socket itself, but the world it filled is
    -- this script's to clear.
    net.disconnect()
    feed = {}
    hurt = 0
    safe_ticks = 0
    -- The next seat is a different pilot's switch, and inheriting this one's
    -- would click on arrival. The kit goes with it: a pad wearing the last
    -- zone's add-ons is a drawing of somebody else's ship.
    multi_was = nil
    flown_ok = false
    held_kit = nil
    marks.hold(nil)
    -- A box about somebody in the room you just left describes a seat number
    -- that the next room will have dealt to a stranger.
    -- The next zone deals these seats to different pilots.
    for k in pairs(rolls) do rolls[k] = nil end
    fx.reset()
    ui.clear_payouts()
    -- Leaving a zone re-derives the tables from the baseline, so what a spec
    -- id means changes on the way out of one as well as into it.
    world.forget_specs()
    -- An empty world. Nothing is drawn from it, but the core is what the next
    -- connection unpacks a snapshot into, and it must not be holding the last
    -- zone's map and settings when that happens.
    sim.init(0x5eed)
    clear_static()
    me = 0
    ui.help = false
    ui.help_prompt = false
    ui.map = false
    ui.room_ask = nil
    ui.col_sect, ui.col_hull = nil, nil
    ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
    ui.panel_shut()
end

-- Everything that ends a session: a leave, a drop, a give-up on a join. The
-- client is meant to be in a room, so this is a state it passes through rather
-- than one it settles in: `session.seek_tick` dials the next room as soon as
-- there is none.
function go_home(self)
    leave_world()
    self.replay = nil
    self.replay_loading = nil
    self.replay_after = nil
    self.replay_tries = nil
    self.online = false
    self.joining = nil
    watching = false
    -- The rooms of the zone being left, and a question about one of them. The
    -- panel is deliberately left standing across a move within a zone, which
    -- is why it has to be put away here: the next zone's list is a different
    -- list, and its scroll offset and its open question belong to nobody.
    ui.rooms_open = false
    ui.room_scroll = 0
    ui.room_ask = nil
    -- And the menu goes with the room. Leaving lands in the stands of the next
    -- room, by way of the loading screen while it is found: this used to open
    -- the games list, which is a page to pick from rather than a place to be,
    -- and under a front end that is itself a game there is somewhere to put a
    -- player down. The list is a press on MENU away.
    menu.close()
    directory.open(menu.directory)
end

-- Who a ship id belongs to. The server's roster, because those are other
-- people and only it knows their names. Everything that names a pilot goes
-- through here, or the feed ends up reading "ship 5 killed ship 8" in a zone
-- full of people.
local function pilot_name(i)
    local p = net.pilots[i]
    return (p and p.name) or ("ship " .. i)
end

-- The same name, wrapped so a feed line knows which of its parts is a name.
-- The interface says its own words in capitals and quotes everybody else's:
-- a call sign is upper, lower and numeric exactly as its owner has it.
--
-- A kill asks for the identity mark as well. Other notices keep their compact
-- sentence, since a flag line already has a verb that explains why it is in
-- the feed and an arrival is not a combat notification.
local function pilot_named(i, marked)
    local p = net.pilots[i]
    return {pilot_name(i), identity = marked and p
        and (p.ai and "bot" or "human") or nil}
end

-- One line in the notification feed. Kills are what it was built for and are
-- still most of what it carries, but anything worth telling the player about
-- in a sentence belongs here rather than in a second widget: it is already
-- the place they look to find out what just happened.
-- `mine` marks a line as being about this pilot rather than about the room.
-- A phone has room for one line at a time and spends it on these, plus every
-- streak line, which the toast admits by its gleam. A desktop also
-- carries arena arrivals and departures, but kill lines have already been
-- limited to this pilot's own kills and deaths by the network queue.
-- `gleam` marks a line as shimmering rather than sitting in `col`. One thing
-- uses it and one thing should: a streak is the only line here about something
-- that is still going on while it is being read.
local function notify(text, col, mine, gleam)
    table.insert(feed, 1,
                 {text = text, col = col, t = 0, mine = mine, gleam = gleam})
    while #feed > ui.FEED_MAX do table.remove(feed) end
end

-- What the zone announced since the last frame, turned into light, noise and
-- feed lines. Drained per tick rather than per frame: a frame can advance the
-- simulation eight times, and the event list is replaced by each one.
--
-- Kills are most of it and a refusal aimed at this pilot rides along, because
-- the two arrive the same way and `update` has no enclosing local to spare
-- for a fifth drain. See `drain_no_team`.
-- Kills come off the wire rather than out of the local simulation. The local
-- death event still drives the explosion, but as a feed line it printed the
-- same kill once per rollback: prediction runs a few ticks ahead, a snapshot
-- from before the death revives the victim, and the next predicted ticks
-- kill them again. The zone announces each death exactly once, with what it
-- paid, and that message is already how ratings arrive.
-- A side the room would not give us, said once.
--
-- The key that asks checks a full bar before it sends, so every refusal that
-- arrives here is one the client had no way to see coming: a seat taken, a
-- side reaped, or a round that landed while the ask was in the air. Read and
-- cleared, because it answers one press rather than describing a state.
-- See decision 150.
--
-- Said wherever the pilot is looking. The panel is usually still up, since
-- the card steps back onto the sheet and the room answers a moment later, and
-- there the note is the register the client's own refusal already speaks in.
-- A pilot who shut the panel and flew off reads the feed instead, for the
-- reason a dropped ship is said there: an ask that vanished in silence is how
-- an interface teaches somebody to stop trusting it.
--
-- Drained beside the kills rather than in `update`, which has no enclosing
-- local to spare: this needs `notify`, and that is one more than the sixty
-- LuaJIT counts to.
local function drain_no_team()
    if not net.no_team then return end
    if menu.open then
        menu.note = net.no_team
    else
        notify({net.no_team}, nil, true)
    end
    net.no_team = nil
    sfx.ui("ui_deny")
end

local function drain_announced()
    drain_no_team()
    for _, k in ipairs(net.kills) do
        local vn = pilot_named(k.victim, true)
        -- Your own bomb counts as your kill in the ledger, which is correct
        -- and reads as nonsense in a feed line.
        --
        -- What it did to your rating, on the end of the line. A kill used
        -- to print what it paid and stopped when nothing was paid, and the
        -- rating is what took the payment's place: it is the only way two
        -- kills differ now. The wreck floats the same figure for a second
        -- and a half where the kill happened, which a pilot who is looking
        -- somewhere else never sees; the feed holds it for nine.
        -- Two lines out of every feed are about you, and they are the two
        -- worth finding without reading: green when you took somebody, red
        -- when somebody took you. Everything else stays the feed's own color,
        -- because a feed where every line is lit says nothing by lighting one.
        --
        -- Yours outranks theirs when you managed both at once, which is a
        -- pilot who blew themselves up with the kill: that is your kill, and
        -- it is the line you will want to see.
        --
        -- Never while watching. From that seat `me` is the sentinel 255,
        -- which is also the killer on a wall death, so every rock somebody
        -- flew into would have been lit as a kill of your own.
        --
        -- Three now rather than two: the third is a kill you helped with,
        -- which the zone tells you and tells nobody else. It is the same
        -- fight seen one step back, so it goes in the same family of green
        -- one shade down rather than in a color of its own. It can never
        -- collide with the other two, since the core hands an assist to
        -- neither the pilot who finished it nor the one who died.
        local col, mine = nil, false
        if not watching and k.killer == me and k.victim ~= me then
            col, mine = pal.PAID, true
        elseif not watching and k.victim == me then
            col, mine = pal.HURT, true
        elseif not watching and k.assist then
            col, mine = pal.ASSIST, true
        end
        local line = (k.victim == k.killer) and {vn, " killed by self"}
            or (k.killer == 255) and {vn, " flew into the wall"}
            or {pilot_named(k.killer, true), " killed ", vn}
        -- Said in words as well as in color, because your name is nowhere on
        -- this line: a kill of yours and a death of yours both name you, and
        -- a lit line about two other people with nothing on it to say why is
        -- a color a player has to be taught. Appended rather than written
        -- into each of the three shapes above, because it is equally true of
        -- all of them: you can soften somebody who then flies into a wall.
        if k.assist then line[#line + 1] = ", you assisted" end
        -- Which deaths carry a figure at all: the ones that moved this
        -- pilot's rating, which is a kill, a death, or a victim they had
        -- softened. A kill that paid nothing still prints its zero, since
        -- the kill was yours and a silent one reads as a figure that failed
        -- to arrive. Every other death is somebody else's and gets none.
        -- See decision 152.
        --
        -- One test, two places to say it. The figure drifts off the wreck,
        -- where a pilot who has just taken somebody is already looking, and
        -- it goes on the end of the feed line, which is where they look a
        -- moment later and where it is still there to read. Two conditions
        -- would be the same rule written twice, and a kill that floated a
        -- figure the feed disagreed with is worse than either alone.
        local moved = not watching and k.gain
            and (mine or k.gain ~= 0) and k.killer ~= 255
        if moved then
            line[#line + 1] = " " .. ui.signed(k.gain)
            ui.payout(sim.ship_x(k.victim), sim.ship_y(k.victim), k.gain)
        end
        notify(line, col, mine)
    end
    if #net.kills > 0 then net.kills = {} end
end

-- Somebody is on a run.
--
-- Drained after the kills and inserted the same way, so the streak line ends
-- up over the kill that earned it: both arrive on one tick and the feed is
-- newest first. Gold, and shimmering, which is the arena's one exception to
-- "a feed where every line is lit says nothing by lighting one". This is not
-- a report of something that has finished, it is a standing fact about
-- somebody who is still flying, and it is the fact that decides who the room
-- goes after next.
--
-- A phone shows every one of these. Its toast keeps kill lines to this
-- pilot's own, but a streak names the pilot the whole room is about to go
-- after, which any player can act on, so the line is not marked `mine`: the
-- gleam is what carries it through the toast's filter, yours or not.
--
-- The alert is not attenuated and does not care where the pilot is. Every
-- other sound in this game is a thing happening somewhere; this one is an
-- announcement, and an announcement that fades with distance is a rumour.
local function drain_streaks()
    for _, k in ipairs(net.streaks) do
        notify({pilot_named(k.ship, true), " is on a streak"},
               nil, nil, true)
        sfx.ui("streak")
    end
    if #net.streaks > 0 then net.streaks = {} end
end

-- Who arrived and who went. A room that fills up around you while you are
-- flying reads as the game getting harder for no reason, and one that empties
-- reads as everybody having got better at avoiding you.
--
-- Not marked `mine`, so the phone's single line stays for the two facts a
-- pilot mid-fight can act on. Somebody walking in is worth knowing and is not
-- worth taking the one line a thumb-sized screen has.
local function drain_comings()
    for _, c in ipairs(net.comings) do
        notify({{c.name}, c.joined and " joined the arena" or " left the arena"})
    end
    if #net.comings > 0 then net.comings = {} end
end

-- What the words are for each reason the room can take a seat back. The wire
-- carries which, never the sentence, the same as everything else said here.
local BENCHED = {
    [net.WHY_LAG] = "moved to spectator: unstable connection",
    [net.WHY_SAFE] = "moved to spectator: too long in the safe zone",
}

-- The room took the seat, and the pilot did not ask it to.
--
-- Red and marked `mine`, which is the pair the feed reserves for the two lines
-- about your own fight, because this outranks both: there is no fight any
-- more. A phone spends its one line on it for the same reason.
--
-- It has to be said out loud because of what a watcher is shown first. The
-- stands run five seconds behind the room, and somebody who has just been
-- benched lands on a frame with their own hull still flying in it, so the one
-- reading available without a line here is that the game has broken.
local function drain_benched()
    local line = net.benched and BENCHED[net.benched]
    net.benched = nil
    if line then
        notify({line}, pal.HURT, true)
        sfx.ui("ui_deny")
    end
end

local function drain_events()
    -- The perspective every color and sound is judged from. While watching
    -- it is the subject, and 255 when the channel has nobody: a ship index
    -- that matches no one, which the world code treats as "none of these are
    -- yours", the reading a watcher wants.
    local view = eye() or 255
    world.events(view, sfx.play)
    -- Which trigger this pilot last pulled, for the key that has to draw its
    -- recovery. The two cooldowns cannot say: a shot locks both triggers for
    -- the firing weapon's delay, so after a bomb the gun carries a wait that
    -- is real and is not its own. The core's fire event names the weapon,
    -- which is the one place the fact exists.
    --
    -- Told apart by the blast on the round, the way world.lua tells a muzzle
    -- flash from a bomb's: a spec that carries one is a bomb, whatever slot a
    -- zone hung it off. Re-latching the same trigger over a replayed step
    -- costs nothing, since what this decides is which ring is drawn and the
    -- clock on it comes from the state.
    if not watching and me then
        for i = 0, sim.event_count() - 1 do
            local ty, a, b = sim.event_at(i)
            if ty == sim.EV_FIRE and a == me then
                touch.trig_fired = (sim.spec_blast(b) or 0) > 0
                    and sim.TRIG_BOMB or sim.TRIG_GUN
            end
        end
    end
    for _, charge in ipairs(net.charge_events) do
        world.remote_charge(charge.slot, charge.x, charge.y, sfx.play)
    end
    if #net.charge_events > 0 then net.charge_events = {} end
    -- What the events cannot say, because they are this client's own
    -- simulation talking and it simulates one pilot. Both of these read the
    -- state instead, so they go here rather than in the loop below.
    world.charges(view, sfx.play)
    world.shots(view, sfx.play)
    -- And what the events never got to say: kills and detonations that
    -- arrived as a snapshot's state change rather than as anything the local
    -- simulation lived through. Without these a mistimed kill is a hull that
    -- vanishes mid-flight with the bomb still drawn beside it.
    for _, d in ipairs(net.snap_deaths) do
        world.corpse(d.ship, d.vx, d.vy, view, sfx.play)
    end
    for _, w in ipairs(net.snap_blasts) do
        world.late_blast(w, sfx.play)
    end
    if #net.snap_deaths > 0 then net.snap_deaths = {} end
    if #net.snap_blasts > 0 then net.snap_blasts = {} end
    for i = 0, sim.event_count() - 1 do
        local ty, a, b, v = sim.event_at(i)
        if ty == sim.EV_HIT and a == me then
            hurt = 1
        elseif ty == sim.EV_FLAG_TAKE or ty == sim.EV_FLAG_DROP then
            local col = (sim.ship_team(a) == eye_team())
                and pal.FRIEND or pal.ENEMY
            notify({pilot_named(a),
                (ty == sim.EV_FLAG_TAKE and " took a flag"
                 or " dropped a flag")}, col)
        elseif ty == sim.EV_GREEN and a == me then
            -- What the green was. The diamond on the ground says nothing
            -- about what it holds, deliberately, because a pilot deciding
            -- whether it is worth the trip is deciding on the trip; the
            -- moment it is taken that stops being true and the answer is
            -- the only thing they want. See `prize.words`.
            --
            -- Only ever your own: the field is the zone's and a prediction
            -- client settles one hull, so this event exists for the pilot
            -- flying and for nobody else. Somebody else's pickup arrives as
            -- a green that is no longer in the snapshot, which the arena
            -- draws and does not narrate.
            --
            -- Marked `mine`, so a phone shows it, which spends the one
            -- line a thumb-sized screen has. That is the right way to spend
            -- it: what a pilot carries is read off the corner stack, and on
            -- a phone the stack stands down to nothing because the pads
            -- carry the marks instead.
            --
            -- In the gold of what you carry, which is what the corner
            -- stack draws a count in and what the hangar prices a build in.
            -- This is the one line in the column that is about your kit
            -- rather than about the fight or about the room, and it was the
            -- feed's own ink, which is also the arrivals, the departures and
            -- every refusal: the one line here a player wants to catch was
            -- wearing the color of the five they can ignore.
            --
            -- Not a green, which is the color of the thing on the ground and
            -- reads as the wrong one up here. The two greens in this column
            -- are what a kill did to your rating, and the prize green sits
            -- between them at a contrast of 1.4 to 1 against the payout: a
            -- third green would be a third light in one family, told apart
            -- by nobody at a glance. The gold's own neighbor is the streak,
            -- which this file has always said is gold that moves.
            notify({"picked up " .. prize.words(b, v)}, pal.CHARGE_COL, true)
        end
    end
end

-- One predicted tick. The server owns the arena, so this sends buttons and
-- replays its own ship; nothing else in the world is stepped here.
local function step_once()
    local b = my_buttons()
    if net.step(b) then drain_events() end
    -- The safe-zone clock, on the tick rather than the frame, because the room
    -- counts ticks and a frame is not one. A watcher has no hull to be in one,
    -- and a dead pilot is not sitting anywhere: both reset, which is what the
    -- room does with them too.
    -- `net.me` rather than this file's own `me`, which is copied out of it
    -- once a frame and after this loop has already run: a seat that changed
    -- this frame would be read here as the one before it, and the value it
    -- holds while watching is 255, which no `sim.*` accessor will take.
    local seat = net.me
    if net.watching or not net.connected or sim.ship_alive(seat) ~= 1
        or sim.in_safe(seat) ~= true then
        safe_ticks = 0
    else
        safe_ticks = safe_ticks + 1
    end
end

-- Text entry on the web goes through real inputs laid over the fields, because
-- a canvas cannot hold a caret and a phone will not raise a keyboard for
-- something that was never focused. There is none left in this client, which
-- is why nothing here talks to the page but the hand-off at the end of the
-- first drawn frame.
function init(self)
    msg.post(".", "acquire_input_focus")
    -- Browsers and mobile operating systems can take a finger or key without
    -- delivering its release. Nothing held before that boundary is still a
    -- deliberate input when the game gets focus back.
    window.set_listener(function(_, event)
        if event == window.WINDOW_EVENT_FOCUS_LOST
            or event == window.WINDOW_EVENT_ICONFIED then
            net.release_controls()
            held = {}
            tapped = {}
            owner = {}
            touch.release_all()
        end
    end)
    math.randomseed(1)
    -- Before anything can ask for a sound. The components start out pointed at
    -- silence and this is what puts audio behind them.
    sfx.init()
    -- Wanted from the first frame. It will not start until the page has been
    -- touched and the audio device is awake, which is why this is a request
    -- the frame loop keeps making rather than a call that either works or
    -- does not. See sfx.music_tick.
    sfx.music(true)

    layers = {
        -- The static layers are built when the camera leaves its window and
        -- uploaded when the camera leaves its window rather than per frame,
        -- but *drawn* every frame in full, so a capacity is a per-frame cost
        -- and not only memory. That is why these two start at a guess and are
        -- then sized against what the map in front of them actually needs;
        -- see fit_static. They used to be fixed at 32768 and 98304, chosen for
        -- the densest window of the densest map and paid for over every empty
        -- one.
        bg = vec.layer("#bg", TUNE.STATIC_SEED),
        bgglow = vec.layer("#bgglow", TUNE.STATIC_SEED),
        -- The sky, which is drawn under the map: see the render script's
        -- pass order. How much of it is on screen is a property of the window
        -- rather than of the build, so these two are resized to the view when
        -- the viewport message arrives, from world.sky_budget. The figures
        -- here are the opening guess for the frames before one does.
        sky = vec.layer("#sky", 16384),
        skyglow = vec.layer("#skyglow", 6144),
        -- These three go up every frame, whole, however little of them is
        -- used, so the headroom is bandwidth spent on nothing, and these are
        -- sized against what a heavy frame actually measures (fill 2.8k, glow
        -- 3.8k, ui 2.1k) with room to spare rather than by an order of
        -- magnitude. Unlike the sky they do not follow the window: what fills
        -- them is the fight, and how much of that there is belongs to the
        -- room.
        fill = vec.layer("#fill", (world.fight_budget())),
        -- Raised from 8192 with the detailed hulls. A hull costs up to 780
        -- vertices where the old outline and spine cost 134, so the old
        -- ceiling held ten of them and their fire, and an overflow does not
        -- report itself: the strokes past the cap simply stop being drawn.
        --
        -- Raised again when strokes got their falloff, which took the worst
        -- hull from 780 vertices to 1176 and this ceiling from twenty-one
        -- hulls down to thirteen. Chaos seats sixty-four. Back to twenty.
        --
        -- Both figures come from world now rather than being written here.
        -- They used to be an opening guess that the viewport message grew to
        -- the window, because half of what these held was sky; with the sky
        -- gone to its own layers nothing grows them, and a guess that is never
        -- corrected is just a ceiling. This one was 24576 against a fight
        -- allowance of 40960, and a busy frame at 1920 by 1080 overflowed it:
        -- what that draws is not a gap but last frame's geometry, left in the
        -- buffer past where this frame stopped writing.
        glow = vec.layer("#glow", (select(2, world.fight_budget()))),
        -- The same falloff, on an interface that is mostly strokes: measured
        -- at every layout and both toggles, the busiest went from 2.1k
        -- vertices to 3639 against a ceiling of 4096, which is a margin too
        -- thin to hold a scoreboard that grows with the room.
        --
        -- Raised again for the map, which is the whole arena as rectangles and
        -- the one thing here whose size is a property of the zone rather than
        -- of the layout. Merged into greedy blocks the three shipped maps come
        -- to 928, 1376 and 2195 of them, so 24576 holds the worst of those
        -- with the rest of the interface still under it.
        ui = vec.layer("#ui", 24576),
        -- The glass under a button, which is the blurred scene drawn inside
        -- the box that asked for it. A handful of rectangles: the landing has
        -- four, and there is no case where an interface wants dozens, because
        -- a screen of glass is a screen of nothing legible.
        frost = vec.layer("#frost", 384),
    }

    -- A generated call sign, restored if this browser has one already. The
    -- configuration still wins, which is how a test names its clients.
    menu.load_identity()
    -- Whether a card asking to be typed into hands its lines to the page as
    -- input elements. A browser is the only place that can be done and the
    -- only place it is worth doing: a password manager fills a form and
    -- cannot fill a drawing. Everywhere else the client draws the lines and
    -- reads its own keys.
    ui.page_fields = html5 ~= nil
    if html5 then
        local ok, route = pcall(html5.run,
            "window.vwRouteRead ? window.vwRouteRead() : ''")
        if ok and type(route) == "string" and route ~= "" then
            self.deep_route = route
        end
    end
    -- The account secret this device already holds, if any. Logging in waits
    -- until the games list says where the meta-layer is.
    account.load()
    menu.defaults(sys.get_config_string("vectorwake.name", ""),
                  sys.get_config_string("vectorwake.directory", ""))

    sim.init(0x5eed)

    -- A pinned server means go here and do not ask, which is what a test and
    -- an operator's own build want. Everybody else gets the menu.
    local url = sys.get_config_string("vectorwake.server", "")
    if url ~= "" then
        -- The server owns state from here, but the core still needs its map
        -- and settings: prediction runs collision locally between snapshots,
        -- and a snapshot carries state, not rules.
        menu.note = "joining"
        local started = net.connect(
            url, menu.class, sys.get_config_string("vectorwake.name", "pilot"),
            function(why)
                menu.note = why
                go_home(self)
            end)
        if not started then return end
        self.online = true
        self.joining = 0
        self.join_progress = net.join_progress
        return
    end

    -- Nothing is joined yet. `session.seek_tick` dials the game this pilot was
    -- in last as soon as the directory answers, and what stands over that room
    -- is the column, because it has never been put away.
    go_home(self)
    -- Which `go_home` did on its way past. Everywhere else it runs, a player
    -- has just left something and the menu goes with it; here it is the first
    -- thing anybody sees, and a column naming the game, who is in it, and the
    -- key that takes a seat is what a client that has just loaded is for. It
    -- dismisses like any other, and MENU brings it back.
    menu.open = true
end

function final(self)
    window.set_listener(nil)
    touch.release_all()
    msg.post(".", "release_input_focus")
end

-- Whether a ship is burning its engine this frame, which is the only thing on
-- screen that separates a pilot accelerating from one coasting.
--
-- Only your own. Buttons are not in a snapshot, so what anybody else is doing
-- with their engine is not something this client is told.
local function burning(i, mine_buttons, bit)
    return i == me and has(mine_buttons, bit)
end

-- Characters into the card that is asking for them, one at a time through the
-- menu's own rule. A keyboard's keys and a paste both arrive here, so a
-- pasted password is held to the same printable ASCII and the same length a
-- typed one is, and a clipboard carrying a tab or a control byte loses it at
-- the same gate.
local function typed(text)
    local moved = false
    for ch in string.gmatch(text, ".") do
        if menu.type_field(ch) then moved = true end
    end
    return moved
end

-- Bank, read off the heading rather than off the buttons, because buttons are
-- yours alone and everybody's ship should lean into a turn. Watching the
-- heading move is the one signal the client has for every seat.
--
-- The rate is smoothed on about a tenth of a second. Remote headings arrive
-- in snapshot steps, so raw differences are a spike and four zeroes; smoothed
-- they are the turn the pilot is actually making. It also eases your own bank
-- in and out instead of snapping it on with the key.
--
-- Purely visual, per decision 5: nothing here touches the simulation, and a
-- rolled hull's collision radius is whatever the core says it is.
TUNE.TAU = 2 * math.pi
TUNE.FULL_TURN = 3.0        -- rad/s of turn that earns the whole bank;
                             -- an unupgraded hull turns at about pi
-- Radians of roll at that rate. Fifty-four degrees, so a hull holding a turn
-- draws at 58% of its width against the 75% it did: the first pass was a lean
-- you had to be told about. Past about sixty the wings foreshorten faster than
-- the eye reads them as wings and the class stops being legible from its
-- silhouette, which identity.md puts above any of this.
TUNE.MAX_BANK = 0.95

local function ship_roll(i, heading, dt)
    local s = rolls[i]
    if not s then
        s = {h = heading, v = 0}
        rolls[i] = s
    end
    local dh = heading - s.h
    s.h = heading
    if dh > 32768 then dh = dh - 65536
    elseif dh < -32768 then dh = dh + 65536 end
    -- A jump no rudder reaches inside a frame is a respawn or a seat handed
    -- to somebody new, and neither is a turn.
    if dt <= 0 or dh > 4096 or dh < -4096 then
        s.v = 0
        return 0
    end
    local rate = dh / 65536 * TUNE.TAU / dt
    s.v = s.v + (rate - s.v) * (1 - math.exp(-dt / 0.11))
    local k = s.v / TUNE.FULL_TURN
    if k > 1 then k = 1 elseif k < -1 then k = -1 end
    return k * TUNE.MAX_BANK
end

function update(self, dt)
    local ww, wh, density, live = frame.begin(self, dt, net, sfx, menu, sim)
    if not ww then return end

    -- A control waiting for a key takes the whole keyboard, and takes it here,
    -- before anything else in this function has looked at a press. Every key
    -- on the board is an answer while this is up, including the ones that walk
    -- the cursor and the ones that open the map behind the panel: a pilot
    -- putting `map` somewhere new would otherwise watch the map open under the
    -- menu as they did it. Escape is the way out and is bound to nothing,
    -- which is what makes it safe to read first.
    if menu.arming then
        local moved = false
        if tapped[TUNE.CONTROL.panel.menu] then
            moved = menu.cancel_bind()
            self.chord = nil
        else
            -- A chord is read by watching a hand rather than by counting
            -- keys. Everything pressed goes on the list, and the list is the
            -- answer the moment the first of them comes back up: Shift, Tab,
            -- let go, and the binding is Shift+Tab. One key is the same
            -- gesture with one key in it, so there is no second rule for it
            -- and no timer anywhere.
            self.chord = self.chord or {}
            for _, k in ipairs(keys.list) do
                if tapped[hash(k.action)] then
                    self.chord[#self.chord + 1] = k.id
                end
            end
            local done = false
            for _, k in ipairs(keys.list) do
                if lifted[hash(k.action)] then done = true break end
            end
            if done and #self.chord > 0 then
                moved = menu.bind_chord(self.chord)
                self.chord = nil
            end
        end
        if moved then sfx.ui("ui_go") end
        tapped = {}
    elseif self.chord then
        self.chord = nil
    end

    -- Fixed 100 Hz stepping under our own accumulator rather than Defold's
    -- fixed_update: prediction and rollback want this clock, not the physics
    -- timestep, and Defold's physics is off entirely.
    -- Q and W spend a hull's charge slots in order, the way a charge pad
    -- already does on a touchscreen: two keys under the left hand, read across
    -- against a stack read top to bottom. By the slots the hull carries rather
    -- than by absolute slot number, so a hull that carries only some still puts
    -- Q on the first of them.
    --
    -- The hull's slots, not the rows drawn: the corner shows only what is in
    -- hand now, so binding to the drawing would move Q onto your burst the
    -- moment your last repel went, which is a key that spends the wrong thing
    -- exactly when a fight is going badly. Pressing Q with nothing to spend
    -- does nothing, which is what it would have done anyway.
    --
    -- Latched like the pad's tap, and for the same reason: the press has to
    -- survive until a simulation step spends it.
    if not menu.open then
        for i, route in ipairs(TUNE.CONTROL.charge) do
            if tapped[route.action] then
                tapped[route.action] = nil
                local slot = my_slots()[i]
                if slot then key_charge = slot end
            end
        end
    end

    if self.replay then
        accum = accum + math.min(dt, 0.25)
        local steps = 0
        while accum >= TUNE.DT and steps < TUNE.MAX_CATCHUP do
            if not session.playback.step(self.replay, sim) then
                menu.note = "match replay ended early"
                go_home(self)
                break
            end
            drain_events()
            accum = accum - TUNE.DT
            steps = steps + 1
        end
        if steps == TUNE.MAX_CATCHUP then accum = 0 end
    elseif self.online then
        accum = accum + math.min(dt, 0.25) * net.clock_rate()
        local steps = 0
        while accum >= TUNE.DT and steps < TUNE.MAX_CATCHUP do
            -- Read once, before anything asks for the buttons, and cleared
            -- after the step that spends it.
            charge_tap = touch.fired_charge() or key_charge
            key_charge = nil
            step_once()
            charge_tap = nil
            accum = accum - TUNE.DT
            steps = steps + 1
        end
        if steps == TUNE.MAX_CATCHUP then accum = 0 end
    else
        accum = 0
    end

    TUNE.CONTROL.dispatch_panel(
        tapped, TUNE.CONTROL.panel, TUNE.PANEL_HANDLERS, live)
    -- The games list is polled only while it is the thing being read: how busy
    -- each game is moves under a player looking at it, and matters not at all
    -- while they are three levels away setting the volume or in a fight. Told
    -- both ways round, because stopping is what makes the next look start with
    -- a fresh ask rather than with a count from ten minutes ago.
    --
    -- The rooms panel is the second thing that reads it. It lists every room
    -- of this game across every arena server, which is a fact only the
    -- directory holds, and the counts on it are the whole reason somebody has
    -- it open. A panel drawn from the last answer before the join would be a
    -- list of where everybody was a minute ago.
    -- The zone stop is the games list, so an open one is what keeps the list
    -- fresh: a zone that comes up or goes down while somebody is reading it
    -- changes under them rather than sitting at whatever the first ask
    -- returned.
    if menu.open and menu.stop_open() == "zone" then
        directory.tick(dt)
    elseif ui.rooms_open and not menu.open then
        directory.tick(dt)
    elseif not live then
        -- Waiting for a room. The list is what the seek loop dials from, so
        -- nothing arrives until it does.
        directory.tick(dt)
    else
        directory.idle()
    end
    if self.deep_route and session.enter_deep_route(self) then
        -- Not for `live`, which is asked again once the joining pass below has
        -- run, but for `menu.adrift`: a deep link joins or leaves mid-frame
        -- and what reads the flag under here would be reading last frame's.
        frame.live(self, net, sim, menu)
    end
    -- The one note that is a claim about the games list rather than about a
    -- press: "nobody is running X" is set when a join finds the row dead, and
    -- the list goes on refreshing underneath it. A note still standing in red
    -- after the row it accuses has come back is the menu contradicting
    -- itself, so it is checked against the list for as long as it is up.
    if note_zone then
        if menu.note ~= ("nobody is running " .. note_zone) then
            note_zone = nil
        else
            for _, r in ipairs(directory.rows) do
                if r.name == note_zone and r.live then
                    menu.note = nil
                    note_zone = nil
                    break
                end
            end
        end
    end
    -- The arena reads the name off the token, so the menu showing a different
    -- one would be showing a name nobody else can see.
    menu.adopt_account_name()
    -- And when the whole identity has moved under a live seat -- a login, a
    -- reroll, a logout whose fresh guest has landed -- the client rejoins the
    -- same game as whoever it now is. The zone binds a seat's identity once,
    -- at the join, and it stays the truth of the roster and the ratings for
    -- the life of the connection: without this, the scoreboard showed the old
    -- name to everyone and every kill kept crediting the old account. A
    -- change of pilot costs the ship, which is honest, since the old pilot's
    -- bounty and kit were never the new one's to keep.
    if self.online and net.connected and not self.joining
        and net.identity_moved(menu.name, account.account) then
        local j = net.last_join()
        if j then
            session.enter(self, j.url, j.zone, j.wt, j.room, j.watch,
                          j.instance)
        end
    end

    -- What the confirm card on your own game offers depends on which of your
    -- two lives this is, so the menu is told every frame, like the class.
    -- From the connection rather than the local, which syncs further down.
    menu.watching = net.watching
    -- Where this client stands in the room, which is what the players
    -- stop answers with: the side you fly for, by the name the zone gave it,
    -- or nothing at all while you are watching. Told every frame, like the
    -- class, because a side can change under you: the whistle benches
    -- everybody and a room being rebalanced moves bots between sides.
    --
    -- The zone's own word for it. A side the zone will not name reads as the
    -- number it is, which is the same answer the sheet's Team column gives
    -- for a private side somebody else is on.
    menu.side = nil
    if not menu.adrift and not net.watching and net.my_team ~= nil then
        local named = net.my_team_name()
        menu.side = (named ~= "" and named) or ("team " .. net.my_team)
    end

    -- And the loop that keeps this client in a room: with none on screen, it
    -- dials the one this pilot was in last.
    session.seek_tick(self, dt)

    if menu.open then
        menu.tick(dt)
        session.browser.link_pointer(self, html5)
        -- Whether a thumb is driving. The controls page is the one page whose
        -- rows depend on it: there is no board to draw on glass and no key
        -- column to fill in, so the same list comes out as gestures.
        menu.touching = touch.used
        -- The round trip belongs to the connection module. The frame layer
        -- slows its noisy estimate into the stable number of bars the about
        -- page reads, so the menu is handed the bars rather than the packets.
        menu.link_bars = self.link_bars
    end
    if menu.ask then
        -- A card owns the keys while it is up, wherever it stands: the stops
        -- behind it cannot be walked and the key behind it cannot be pressed.
        -- `menu.step` reads its own block for this and needs no column open.
        --
        -- The space bar is not a send: a call sign has a space in it, so the
        -- trigger goes into the line rather than answering the card partway
        -- through a name.
        local act, moved = menu.step({
            left = tapped[hash("k_left")], right = tapped[hash("k_right")],
            up = tapped[hash("k_up")], down = tapped[hash("k_down")],
            go = tapped[hash("select")],
            back = tapped[TUNE.CONTROL.panel.menu],
            rub = tapped[hash("backspace")],
        })
        if moved then sfx.ui(act and "ui_go" or "ui_move") end
        if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
    elseif menu.open then
        -- What is on the screen is a fact the drawing has already decided:
        -- `ui.col_walk` reads the boxes the last frame published rather than
        -- a second list of controls kept beside it.
        --
        -- Up and down move the one cursor a pointer moves, and enter presses
        -- what it is on, which with nothing lit is the column's own key. See
        -- `ui.col_step` and `ui.col_go`.
        --
        -- Left and right are read where a row holds a value: the ship panel's
        -- credits, and the settings panel's ranges. `ui.col_side` is what
        -- knows which, so this stays one branch.
        local dir = (tapped[hash("k_down")] and 1)
            or (tapped[hash("k_up")] and -1) or 0
        if dir ~= 0 and ui.col_step(dir) then sfx.ui("ui_move") end
        local side = (tapped[hash("k_right")] and 1)
            or (tapped[hash("k_left")] and -1) or 0
        if side ~= 0 then
            local action, value = ui.col_side(side)
            if action then menu_act(self, action, value) end
        end
        -- Enter or space presses what the cursor is on. Space is a key a
        -- pilot may fly on and cannot be a second enter there, but nothing
        -- in this branch is flying: it runs only while a column or a panel
        -- is the thing on screen, and space is what a hand expects to press
        -- a row with on every other interface it has used.
        --
        -- Not while a card is taking type, which is the branch above: a call
        -- sign has a space in it, and the trigger belongs to the line.
        if tapped[hash("select")] or tapped[hash("k_space")] then
            local action, value = ui.col_go()
            if action then menu_act(self, action, value) end
        end
    end

    -- And what the pointer is resting on, which turns the engine's y, counting
    -- up, into the interface's, counting down.
    --
    -- Out here rather than beside it, because the corner is what a pilot looks
    -- at while flying and the block above runs only while the menu is up.
    -- Written in there, this nilled itself on its own first line every frame it
    -- ran in, so the card could not appear at all and the tests could not see
    -- it: they set `ui.hover_x` directly, which is the one thing this decides.
    --
    -- Nil while the menu is open or a thumb is driving: a card explaining the
    -- corner under a panel that covers it is a card nobody asked for, and glass
    -- has no pointer to rest.
    if menu.open or touch.used or not self.cursor_x then
        ui.hover_x, ui.hover_y = nil, nil
    else
        ui.hover_x = self.cursor_x
        ui.hover_y = (self.vh or 0) - self.cursor_y
    end
    -- And the same question of the front end's own boxes, which are not the
    -- corner's and not the menu's: the landing is the one screen with things
    -- to press on it while the drawer is shut.
    if land_hover(self.cursor_x, self.cursor_y, self.vh or 0) then
        sfx.ui("ui_move")
    end

    tapped = {}
    lifted = {}
    pointer_took = nil

    if self.replay then
        me = 255
        watching = true
    elseif self.online and net.connected then
        me = net.me
        -- Whatever the thumbs were holding when the cockpit went away, let go
        -- of it. on_input stops feeding touch.lua for the length of a watch,
        -- so a release landing after this point is never delivered and the
        -- latch it was meant to clear survives: the pad held while tapping
        -- WATCH still reads as held, silently, until a hull is taken again and
        -- the ship opens fire with nobody's finger on the glass.
        if net.watching and not watching then touch.release_all() end
        watching = net.watching
    end

    -- A seat just taken has the hull's own profile on it, so tell the arena
    -- what this pilot actually spent their credits on. Outside the joining
    -- branch below, because a seat is also taken from the stands halfway
    -- through a session, where nothing is joining any more.
    if net.owes_build then
        net.owes_build = false
        menu.send_build(menu.class)
    end

    -- A zone that accepts the socket but never sends a snapshot is as broken
    -- as one that refuses it, and on the web a connection to a dead port can
    -- hang without ever raising an error. Either way the player gets the menu
    -- back with a reason on it rather than a black arena.
    if self.online and self.joining then
        if self.join_progress ~= net.join_progress then
            self.join_progress = net.join_progress
            self.joining = 0
        end
        if net.connected and net.stats.snaps > 0 then
            self.joining = nil
            menu.note = nil
            -- The board, offered once, and only to a client that arrived
            -- holding a hull. A watcher has no controls to be told about, and
            -- most arrivals are watchers now: `session.take_seat` makes the
            -- same offer at the moment a seat is taken.
            --
            -- Nothing else goes out behind the join. A kit used to: the room
            -- dealt a starter kit to any seat wearing nothing and the pilot's
            -- own build lived in the account, so it had to be sent or an
            -- evening in the hangar was lost. The hull carries its own profile
            -- now and the room deals it, so the class byte in the join is the
            -- whole of what this client has to say about the ship.
            if not net.watching then
                ui.help_prompt = menu.needs_help_prompt()
            end
        else
            self.joining = self.joining + dt
            if self.joining > 10 then
                menu.note = "no answer from the zone"
                go_home(self)
            end
        end
    end

    -- Asked again, because the answer can have changed since the top of the
    -- frame. A menu row can join a zone, leave one, or give up on a join, and
    -- all three tear the world down mid-frame: joining calls `sim.init` and
    -- leaves nothing to draw until the first snapshot. Carrying the old answer
    -- down to the drawing below would read the empty core as though a game were
    -- still in it, and rebuild terrain around tile zero on the way past.
    live = frame.live(self, net, sim, menu)

    -- What the zone announced since last frame, and who came and went with
    -- it, before the expiry pass so a line's clock starts the frame it
    -- appears.
    drain_announced()
    drain_streaks()
    drain_comings()
    drain_benched()

    -- Notifications expire. A feed that only ever scrolls leaves the last few
    -- deaths on screen through a lull, which reads as a fight that is still
    -- happening somewhere.
    for i = #feed, 1, -1 do
        feed[i].t = feed[i].t + dt
        if feed[i].t > ui.FEED_LIFE then table.remove(feed, i) end
    end

    -- And so do the phrases. Same shape as the feed above and for the same
    -- reason: a "gg" still on the card two matches later is a chat log, and
    -- this is not one.
    for ship, said in pairs(net.said) do
        said.t = said.t + dt
        if said.t > ui.SAY_LIFE then net.said[ship] = nil end
    end

    kb_locked = frame.sample(self, dt, net, html5, kb_locked)

    -- Where this frame falls between the two ticks either side of it, and how
    -- much of the last correction has been walked off. Everything that reads a
    -- position below gets both folded in, including the camera, so the whole
    -- scene moves together rather than the world sliding under a hull that is
    -- being smoothed on its own.
    --
    -- Set even when there is no game: the menu's drifting starfield does not
    -- ask the core for anything, but a stale alpha left over from the last
    -- arena would be waiting for the next one.
    sim.render_alpha(accum / TUNE.DT)
    if live then sim.smooth_decay(dt) end
    if live and ui.debug then
        net.stats.smooth_pos, net.stats.smooth_turn =
            sim.smooth_stats(watching and -1 or me)
    end
    local cx, cy
    if live then
        -- What the menu calls your hull is whatever you are flying, asked
        -- every frame rather than remembered: the server decides, and it can
        -- refuse a change the menu has already drawn as chosen. A watcher has
        -- no hull; the menu keeps showing the one they would fly back in.
        --
        -- Unless a ship is being drafted, which is the one time the menu's
        -- hull is ahead of the ship rather than behind it: the panel is
        -- showing what the pilot is building, and this would drag it back
        -- onto what they are still flying on every frame.
        if not watching and not menu.drafting() then
            menu.class = sim.ship_class(me)
        end
        -- The camera stands behind the eye: your hull, or the subject the
        -- snapshot named. An empty channel holds the middle of the map.
        local e = eye()
        if e then
            cx, cy = sim.ship_x(e), sim.ship_y(e)
        else
            cx, cy = 512 * 16, 512 * 16
        end
        -- Terrain follows the camera. A map this size cannot be meshed whole,
        -- so the window is rebuilt once the camera has walked far enough that
        -- its edge is getting close to the screen.
        local ctx, cty = math.floor(cx / 16), math.floor(cy / 16)
        if not static.tx or math.abs(ctx - static.tx) >= TUNE.STATIC_STEP
           or math.abs(cty - static.ty) >= TUNE.STATIC_STEP then
            rebuild_static(ctx, cty)
        end
    else
        -- Nothing to look at, so the camera drifts and the starfield moves
        -- under the panel. A still field reads as a screenshot of a game
        -- rather than a game waiting to be picked.
        self.drift = (self.drift or 0) + dt * 7
        cx, cy = 512 * 16 + self.drift, 512 * 16 + self.drift * 0.4
    end

    sfx.listener(cx, cy)
    fx.listener(cx, cy)
    fx.update(dt)
    hurt = hurt * (0.955 ^ (dt * 60))

    local sx, sy = fx.shake_offset()
    msg.post("@render:", "camera",
             {position = vmath.vector3(cx + sx, cy + sy, 0)})

    -- A heartbeat, which is the only way to see anything in a headless
    -- build and cheap enough to leave in.
    self.report = (self.report or 0) + dt
    if self.report > 5 then
        self.report = 0
        if live then
            -- Name every pilot the server says is here. The roster is the
            -- point: it is how you know somebody else showed up, and it is the
            -- first thing to break when they do.
            local who = {}
            for i = 0, sim.ship_count() - 1 do
                local p = net.pilots[i]
                who[#who + 1] = (p and p.name or ("?" .. i))
                    .. ((p and p.ai) and "*" or "")
            end
            print(string.format("tick %d  ships %d  err %.2fpx  roster %s",
                sim.tick(), sim.ship_count(), net.stats.self_err,
                table.concat(who, " ")))
        else
            print(string.format("menu  zone %s  games %d  %s",
                menu.zone == "" and "-" or menu.zone,
                #directory.rows, menu.note or directory.note or ""))
        end
    end

    -- --- the world -------------------------------------------------------
    local fill, glow = layers.fill, layers.glow
    local sky, skyglow = layers.sky, layers.skyglow
    fill:reset()
    glow:reset()
    sky:reset()
    skyglow:reset()

    -- The sky first, and into layers of its own: they move against the camera,
    -- so they cannot live in the mesh the walls are baked into, and the render
    -- script draws them under the map so a wall is a hole in the sky rather
    -- than a pane of glass with stars behind it. It is the whole of the home
    -- screen's backdrop and the bottom of the arena's, which is one routine
    -- either way.
    world.stars(sky, skyglow, cx + sx, cy + sy, self.hw, self.hh)

    -- Nothing over the starfield on the home screen. A hull used to cross it
    -- on a closed loop, trailing a wake, on the argument that a text column in
    -- the middle of an empty field carried nothing. What it actually did was
    -- put a ship nobody is flying in front of a menu about flying one, moving
    -- against a field that is already moving. The field on its own is the
    -- picture: it drifts, it is the same sky the game is played under, and
    -- there is nothing on it pretending to be a game.

    if live then
        -- A zone's map replaces whatever was there, and the terrain mesh is
        -- built on the assumption that never happens. Rebuilt here rather than
        -- where the message lands: this is the phase that owns the layers, and
        -- a static buffer refilled from the middle of a frame does not survive
        -- it.
        if net.map_epoch ~= (self.map_epoch or 0) then
            self.map_epoch = net.map_epoch
            rebuild_static()
            -- The sky is the map's as much as the walls are: the band's angle
            -- and where the two washes sit are both placed from the name, so a
            -- room looks like itself every time it is played and like nowhere
            -- else.
            world.sky_seed(net.map_name)
            -- The map view is the one thing that reads all thousand tiles, so
            -- it is built here with the terrain and never again. Doing it on
            -- the first press instead would put a scan of the whole arena in
            -- the frame somebody asked to see it.
            world.build_overview()
        end
        -- Same reason, for what a weapon looks like: a zone's spec table is
        -- its own, so a bolt here can be a bomb there, and the drawing code
        -- caches that answer per spec id.
        if net.settings_epoch ~= (self.settings_epoch or 0) then
            self.settings_epoch = net.settings_epoch
            world.forget_specs()
            -- And the terrain with it, because a setting decides part of what
            -- the terrain is: whether the map's spawn tiles carry a mark. The
            -- mesh is built once every sixteen tiles of camera travel, so
            -- without this a zone that turns the marks off keeps showing them
            -- until somebody flies far enough for the window to be rebuilt.
            rebuild_static()
        end

        local t = sim.tick() / TUNE.TICK_HZ
        local my_team = eye_team()
        local mine_buttons = my_buttons()

        -- The last five seconds of whichever clock is running, said out
        -- loud. The rule is net.lua's, beside the message it reads; this is
        -- the two pieces of state it needs and the sound it asks for.
        --
        -- The state rides on `self` rather than beside `counted_at` up top,
        -- because this file has one function holding every local in it and
        -- Lua stops a closure at sixty enclosing ones. `upvalues_test` is
        -- what says so.
        local rang
        rang, counted_at, self.was_playing =
            net.clock_says(counted_at, self.was_playing)
        if rang == "tick" then
            sfx.ui("count")
        elseif rang == "start" then
            sfx.ui("start")
        end

        -- Held thrust is the one sound with a duration rather than an instant,
        -- so it is a loop the frame turns on and off rather than an event.
        -- Edges only: playing a looping component again starts a second voice.
        sfx.loop("thrust", not watching and sim.ship_alive(me) == 1
            and not menu.open
            and (has(mine_buttons, sim.BTN_THRUST)
                 or has(mine_buttons, sim.BTN_REVERSE)))

        -- Declining the fan, or taking it back. The only control in this game
        -- whose whole effect is a setting: nothing moves and nothing is spent,
        -- so the sound is most of what says the key landed.
        --
        -- Read off the ship rather than off the press, because the core owns
        -- the edge and this way there is one answer instead of two that can
        -- disagree. That is also what keeps a hull carrying no fan silent: the
        -- core refuses the press outright, so there is no change here to hear.
        -- Once a frame, not once a tick: a snapshot rolls the ship back and
        -- replays it, and the flicker inside that belongs to the rollback
        -- rather than to anything a pilot did.
        --
        -- One change here is nobody's press: losing the last fan puts the
        -- switch back, and a pilot who has just died does not need a click
        -- about their weapons on the way out. So the sound needs a fan in
        -- hand as well as a change, which the press always has and the
        -- clearing never does.
        if not watching then
            local multi_now = sim.ship_multi_off(me)
            local have_fan =
                sim.ship_mod(me, sim.TRIG_GUN, sim.MOD_MULTI) > 0
                or sim.ship_mod(me, sim.TRIG_BOMB, sim.MOD_MULTI) > 0
            if multi_was ~= nil and multi_now ~= multi_was and have_fan then
                -- The two interface sounds this client already has, used the
                -- way the menu uses them: the rising one for taking something,
                -- the tick for stepping a control along.
                sfx.ui(multi_now and "ui_move" or "ui_go")
            end
            multi_was = multi_now
        end

        world.flags(fill, glow, my_team, t)
        -- What is on screen, grown by enough that nothing pops at the edge: a
        -- bomb's halo is 13 px, a hull's is 26, a blast reaches further.
        -- Drawing the rest of a thousand-tile map is not merely wasted work,
        -- it overflows the layer and the primitives that fall past the cap
        -- simply vanish, which reads as hulls changing shape and energy bars
        -- blinking empty rather than as a slow frame.
        local cull = {x0 = cx - self.hw - 96, x1 = cx + self.hw + 96,
                      y0 = cy - self.hh - 96, y1 = cy + self.hh + 96}
        -- Light is collected before anything is drawn, because a hull lit by
        -- a blast has to know about it while drawing itself, and effects are
        -- drawn last of all. The walls read the same list at the end of the
        -- frame, by which point the weapons and the engines have added
        -- theirs.
        world.lights_begin()
        fx.gather_lights(world.light)
        world.greens(fill, glow, t, cull)
        world.weapons(fill, glow, t, cull)

        for i = 0, sim.ship_count() - 1 do
            -- Yours is always drawn: the camera is on it, so it cannot be off
            -- screen, and a frame that skipped it would be a frame with no
            -- ship.
            if sim.ship_alive(i) == 1
               and (i == me or not (sim.ship_x(i) < cull.x0 or sim.ship_x(i) > cull.x1
                                    or sim.ship_y(i) < cull.y0 or sim.ship_y(i) > cull.y1)) then
                local col = (sim.ship_team(i) == my_team) and pal.FRIEND or pal.ENEMY
                -- Plates, panel lines and lamps only within the fight you are
                -- actually in. A hull draws the same size wherever it is on
                -- screen, so this is not a level of detail in the usual sense;
                -- it is a cap on what a dogpile can ask of the glow layer,
                -- which does not report an overflow, it just stops drawing.
                local dx = sim.ship_x(i) - cx
                local dy = sim.ship_y(i) - cy
                local hdg = sim.ship_heading(i)
                -- The ribbon, from this hull's own exhaust, on the heading
                -- the hull is drawn at rather than the one the core holds.
                -- Whose ribbon wears the pilot's wake choice. Told here
                -- because only this file knows which ship is the player's.
                world.wake_of = (not watching) and me or nil
                world.wake_style = menu.wake or 0
                world.trail(glow, i, sim.ship_x(i), sim.ship_y(i), hdg,
                            sim.ship_class(i), col, t)
                -- How hurt, on the same number the pip below reads. Nothing
                -- until under a bar's worth of energy is gone, so a hull
                -- that has been scratched once still draws clean and the
                -- grading means something when it starts.
                local frac = world.energy_fraction(i)
                local wound = frac < TUNE.HURT_AT
                    and (1 - frac / TUNE.HURT_AT) or 0
                world.ship(fill, glow, sim.ship_class(i), sim.ship_x(i),
                           sim.ship_y(i), hdg, col, {
                    mine = i == me,
                    far = dx * dx + dy * dy > TUNE.DETAIL_R2,
                    thrusting = burning(i, mine_buttons, sim.BTN_THRUST),
                    reversing = burning(i, mine_buttons, sim.BTN_REVERSE),
                    flicker = (sim.tick() * 7 + i * 13) % 11 / 11,
                    roll = ship_roll(i, hdg, dt),
                    hurt = wound,
                    flash = world.hit_flash(i),
                    -- The clock this hull's shimmer runs on, and nil for a
                    -- hull that is not on a run. Offset by the seat so two
                    -- streaking pilots are a fraction out of step: in step
                    -- they read as one effect drawn twice.
                    gleam = sim.ship_on_streak(i) and (t + i * 0.31) or nil,
                })
                -- Only the wounded carry a pip. A bar over every hull in a
                -- nine-ship room is noise; a bar over the one that is hurt is
                -- the single most useful thing on screen, and its appearing
                -- is itself the notice that something hit you, which is why it
                -- is absent at full rather than merely empty.
                --
                -- Your own grades from team color into the hurt colors as it
                -- falls. That used to be the corner bar's job and the corner
                -- bar is gone: without this a pilot at eight percent and a
                -- pilot at ninety would be told the same thing in the same
                -- color, and the length of a 22-pixel bar is not a warning.
                if frac < 0.995 then
                    local bar_col = col
                    if i == me then
                        if frac < 0.2 then bar_col = pal.HURT
                        elseif frac < 0.45 then bar_col = pal.ENEMY end
                    end
                    world.ship_bar(fill, glow, sim.ship_x(i), sim.ship_y(i), frac,
                                   bar_col)
                end

            end
        end

        -- Scenery that belongs above the ships, now that they are drawn.
        world.draw_over(glow, cull)

        -- A zone can remove a hull's bomb rack, so the pad that fires one may
        -- have nothing to fire. Told every frame rather than on the hull
        -- change, because a zone can hand you a different ship than the one
        -- you asked for.
        -- What the marks read this frame. Set before anything draws one,
        -- because the pads, the corner stack and the charge rows all ask and
        -- a frame where some of them had the live ship and some the copy
        -- would be the disagreement this exists to prevent. Never while
        -- watching: the hull under the camera is somebody else's, and its
        -- kit is theirs to lose rather than yours to keep.
        held_kit = not watching and loadout_held() or nil
        marks.hold(held_kit)
        touch.has_bomb = not watching and sim.has_trigger(me, sim.TRIG_BOMB)
        -- Which hull is yours, so a pad can read its own weapon's level and
        -- add-ons out of the core instead of having each of them copied into
        -- a field here and kept in step. Nil while watching: every touch.lua
        -- read of it already guards, and the pads it feeds are not drawn.
        touch.me = not watching and me or nil
        -- Where the dial ends, so the charge rail knows what it may not climb
        -- into. ui.lua owns that metric and cannot be asked from touch.lua,
        -- which it requires.
        touch.ceiling = wh - ui.radar_span()
        -- Which charge slots this hull carries, and how many of each are in
        -- hand. The pads are built from this, so a hull with none draws none
        -- and a zone that fills all four gets four.
        touch.charges = my_slots()
        touch.counts = {}
        touch.maxes = {}
        touch.waits = {}
        touch.delays = {}
        for _, k in ipairs(touch.charges) do
            touch.counts[k] = held_kit and held_kit.charge[k]
                or sim.ship_charge(me, k)
            touch.maxes[k] = sim.ship_kit(me, sim.SLOT_CHARGE0 + k)
            touch.waits[k] = sim.ship_charge_wait(me, k)
            touch.delays[k] = sim.charge_delay(k)
        end
        -- And the same pair per trigger, which is what a key's rim grows back
        -- over after a shot. Per trigger rather than the higher of the two:
        -- the counters do come apart, an EMP bomb leaving its own guns
        -- running, and a rim on the gun answering the bomb's clock would be
        -- wrong in exactly the fight where it matters.
        --
        -- Guarded like every other read of `me` on this frame, and for a
        -- harder reason than the keys not being drawn: watching sets `me` to
        -- 255, the wire's "nobody", which the core refuses as a ship index, so
        -- an unguarded read raised before the layers flushed and the screen
        -- sat frozen on the last frame the watcher had flown.
        touch.trig_waits = {}
        touch.trig_delays = {}
        if not watching then
            for _, trig in ipairs({sim.TRIG_GUN, sim.TRIG_BOMB}) do
                touch.trig_waits[trig] = sim.ship_trigger_wait(me, trig)
                touch.trig_delays[trig] = sim.ship_trigger_delay(me, trig)
            end
        end

        -- Doors and wormholes: world geometry that moves, so it cannot live in
        -- the static mesh the way the walls do.
        world.draw_tiles(fill, glow, t, cull)
        fx.draw(glow)
        world.wall_light(glow)
    else
        sfx.loop("thrust", false)
    end

    sky:flush()
    skyglow:flush()
    fill:flush()
    glow:flush()

    -- --- the interface ---------------------------------------------------
    -- The page measures the insets in CSS pixels; the interface and the pads
    -- lay out in drawable ones, so the density converts here, in the one
    -- place both readers are told.
    ui.safe((self.safe_l or 0) * density, (self.safe_r or 0) * density,
            (self.safe_t or 0) * density, (self.safe_b or 0) * density,
            self.installed)
    touch.safe_l = (self.safe_l or 0) * density
    touch.safe_r = (self.safe_r or 0) * density
    -- The world canvas reaches the whole display even when iOS reports a
    -- shorter visual viewport. Glass controls step above that extension, so
    -- growing the battlefield does not move either thumb's resting place.
    touch.safe_b = (self.safe_b or 0) * density
    -- The same four numbers, kept for the about page to print. Every device
    -- that has ever got this wrong has been a phone, and a phone is exactly
    -- where they cannot be read off a console: the emulator answers zero to
    -- all four, so the only place the real ones exist is a screen in
    -- somebody's hand.
    menu.screen = {w = ww, h = wh, d = density,
                   l = self.safe_l or 0, r = self.safe_r or 0,
                   t = self.safe_t or 0, b = self.safe_b or 0,
                   app = self.installed,
                   vl = self.vp_layout or 0, vv = self.vp_visual or 0,
                   vi = self.vp_inner or 0, vo = self.vp_outer or 0,
                   vs = self.vp_screen or 0, vt = self.vp_top or 0}
    ui.begin(layers.ui, ww, wh, density, touch.used, self.clock or 0,
             layers.frost)
    -- The interface stays up under the menu. Nothing is paused, so hiding the
    -- scoreboard and the radar behind a panel would be a lie about what is
    -- happening: you can be shot while reading this.
    --
    -- Behind the landing as well, which is the whole of the idea: what a
    -- stranger opening the client meets is the game, drawn by the code that
    -- draws it to the people in it. The watcher's HUD already has the shape
    -- for it, carrying the room's instruments and none of the hull's
    -- furniture, so the landing is that HUD with two things added at the
    -- foot, and the deck that used to describe a room from outside it is
    -- gone.
    if live then
        local e = eye()
        -- The perspective seat: yours, or the watched subject's, and nil when
        -- the channel has nobody to stand behind. Written out rather than as
        -- `watching and e or me`, which is the same shape as every other
        -- branch in this file and wrong for exactly one case: an `and` whose
        -- right side is nil falls through to the `or`, so a watcher with no
        -- subject was handed 255 and the interface spent the whole warm-up of
        -- the delayed channel asking the core about a seat that cannot exist.
        local hud_me = me
        if watching then hud_me = e end
        if hud_me and sim.ship_private and not sim.ship_private(hud_me) then hud_me = nil end
        ui.hud({
            me = hud_me,
            -- Watching, and what of: a particular hull, or the room's own
            -- feed. The subject alone cannot say, because the channel is
            -- usually pointed at somebody too. What separates them is whether
            -- the hull on screen is the one this client asked for and whether
            -- the zone could have granted it, which is your own side.
            --
            -- Written as a branch rather than `a and b or c`: the middle can
            -- be nil here, and that shape falls through to the wrong answer
            -- when it is.
            watch = watch_state(),
            -- The call sign belongs to this connection even while the camera
            -- belongs to a watched pilot. The roster uses it to mark this
            -- watcher's own row instead of marking the camera subject as us.
            viewer_name = menu.name,
            -- The side this screen belongs to. Not the subject's: watching,
            -- the camera stands behind whoever the channel picked, and the
            -- question every instrument asks is which end of the gun you are
            -- on rather than which end they are.
            side = eye_team(),
            -- You are the channel's subject: the camera is on you, and the
            -- mark that says so is the one courtesy the channel owes.
            on_air = net.on_air,
            watchers = net.watchers,
            -- Whether the column is standing over the room, open or still on
            -- its way down. It is drawn for as long as it is on screen, and
            -- the two big centered things the HUD draws, the ending and the
            -- help table, have to stay stood down for exactly that long.
            menu_open = menu.open or ui.column_up(),
            -- And whether one of its stops has a panel open, which is a
            -- different question: names scattered through the glass read as a
            -- fault, so the plates and the phone's feed go down under a panel
            -- wherever it stands. The bare column is four rows at the foot and
            -- covers nothing.
            panel = menu.open and menu.stop_open() ~= nil,
            -- And whether a question is standing over all of it, which is the
            -- one thing on screen that takes the keys off everything else.
            card = menu.ask ~= nil,
            -- The server's roster, because those are other people's names and
            -- only it knows them.
            pilots = net.pilots,
            -- Live and separate from the pilot rows, because it moves on a
            -- different beat: a roster arrives when somebody joins or leaves,
            -- and a rating changes on every rated death between two of them.
            ratings = net.ratings,
            -- And what everybody was rated at when the match opened, which
            -- the podium subtracts from the live figure to say what the
            -- match did. Only the ending reads it: mid-fight a rating is a
            -- standing rather than a running total, and a number climbing
            -- over somebody's head while they are trying to fly is the shape
            -- the bounty had.
            rated_from = net.rated_from,
            -- The sides, as the zone is willing to describe them. A pilot's
            -- box names the one they are on only when the zone marked it
            -- public, so what may be said comes from here rather than from a
            -- team byte the client could read off any ship.
            teams = net.teams,
            -- The clock and the score, from a zone that plays matches, and
            -- the sides' own names to draw beside them. Nil in a room that
            -- runs forever, which is how the HUD decides whether to draw a
            -- clock at all.
            match = net.match,
            side_names = side_names(),
            -- The closed list of phrases and who has just used one. Both come
            -- from the connection: the list is the wire's own, since a number
            -- on it is what travels, and the table is what the zone has said
            -- since the whistle.
            sayings = net.SAYINGS, said = net.said,
            -- Whether that box should also offer to invite whoever it is
            -- describing, and who has already been asked. Both are the
            -- connection's business, so they are read here rather than in the
            -- renderer, which knows about drawing and not about sides.
            may_invite = net.may_invite(), invited = net.invited,
            feed = feed, hurt = hurt,
            -- How long this pilot has been sitting in a safe zone and how
            -- long the room allows, so the interface can say both. Zero for a
            -- limit means the room never takes a seat back and there is no
            -- countdown to draw, only the label.
            safe = safe_ticks, safe_limit = sim.safe_limit(),
            -- How far up the screen the thumb's controls climb, so the one
            -- feed line a phone shows can be placed clear of them. Measured
            -- from the same layout the taps are tested against, because the
            -- hand is as tall as the charges a hull happens to carry.
            pad_top = touch.used and pad_reach(ww, wh, density) or nil,
            -- What the stick's rim has to say, and where. The one control on
            -- glass whose meaning a mark cannot carry, so it is written in
            -- words, and words come from the gui: touch.lua works out the
            -- sentence and the place, this hands it over, ui.lua draws it.
            --
            -- Under the same conditions the stick itself is drawn under, and
            -- the list is worth keeping in step by hand rather than shortening:
            -- a caption for a control nobody is being shown is a word sitting
            -- on the menu, on the ending board, or over somebody else's fight.
            stick_hint = (touch.used and live and not menu.open and not watching
                          and not ui.match_ended(net.match))
                and touch.hint(ww, wh, density) or nil,
            charges = charge_row(),
            cam_x = cx, cam_y = cy,
            half_w = self.hw, half_h = self.hh,
            -- Flags are drawn as flags now, so the banner is only for the
            -- things that are genuinely a sentence.
            banner = net.banner,
            lag_notice = net.lag_notice,
            stats = net.stats, zone = net.zone,
            -- How the line is, in bars, for the meter over the dial. The round
            -- trip belongs to the connection module and the frame layer slows
            -- its noisy estimate into a stable count, so what the HUD is handed
            -- is the reading rather than the packets.
            link = self.link_bars,
            -- Every room of this game and which one we are in, for the chip
            -- in the corner and the panel it opens. Both are absent until the
            -- directory says there is more than one.
            rooms = my_rooms(), room = net.room,
            fps = self.fps, frame_ms = self.frame_ms,
            rx_rate = self.rx_rate, tx_rate = self.tx_rate,
            -- The two world layers themselves, for the debug readout: how
            -- full each one is and whether it refused anything. A layer past
            -- its capacity does not raise, it stops drawing, so the only way
            -- to find out used to be to notice the missing geometry and guess
            -- why. The numbers are last frame's, since the counts are settled
            -- by the flush that happens after this is drawn.
            layers = layers,
        })
    end
    -- No room to draw: the loader's own picture, held until one answers. It
    -- is what the page was already showing, so the hand-off from the loading
    -- screen to the engine changes nothing anybody can see, and the wait for
    -- a directory and a handshake happens behind a wordmark rather than
    -- behind a menu.
    if not live then
        -- Only what has gone wrong. A normal wait is a directory lookup and a
        -- handshake, two seconds of it, and a caption narrating that is a
        -- label on the one screen that should look like the game. What earns
        -- the line is a join that failed, or a directory that has answered and
        -- named no games: silence there is a client that looks like it is
        -- still trying when it has already finished and found nothing.
        local why = menu.note
        if (not why or why == "") and directory.answered then
            why = directory.note
        end
        ui.waiting(why)
    end
    -- The ship panel coming up and going away, which is where a draft starts
    -- and where it is settled.
    --
    -- Watched here rather than hung off each press that can close it. There
    -- are six of those: the way back on the head, the stop pressed again, the
    -- column's key, escape, the glass beside the panel, and a socket that
    -- dropped. A draft that outlived any one of them would be a menu holding
    -- a ship the pilot had walked away from.
    -- The whistle, which raises the players sheet by itself.
    --
    -- There is no ending page any more. The account of a match that just
    -- finished is the sheet, and what the sheet gains at the whistle is the
    -- rating column: the match's own arithmetic, which is the one thing about
    -- it a mid-fight list has no business carrying. Who took it is said by
    -- the band, in the pixels that have carried the score for three minutes.
    -- See decision 147.
    --
    -- Raised the way a hand raises it, so escape, a press beside it and the
    -- menu key all put it away exactly as they do the rest of the time.
    -- Put away again when the next match opens, but only if nobody has walked
    -- off it: a pilot who went to the ship stop during the intermission keeps
    -- the panel they went to, and one who is still reading the sheet when the
    -- whistle for the next match goes gets their fight back.
    local ended = ui.match_ended(net.match) and net.connected
    if ended ~= self.whistle_up then
        self.whistle_up = ended
        if ended then
            self.whistle_raised = open_players()
        else
            if self.whistle_raised and menu.open
               and menu.stop_open() == "players" then
                menu.close()
                ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
            end
            self.whistle_raised = false
        end
    end
    -- The ship panel coming up, which is where a draft starts. Nothing
    -- happens when it goes: the draft stands on the column, the ship stop
    -- names what is pending, and the key is what spends it. See decision 157.
    local ship_up = menu.open and menu.stop_open() == "ship"
    if ship_up and not self.ship_panel_up then menu.draft_open() end
    self.ship_panel_up = ship_up
    -- The panel, and the slide it leaves on.
    --
    -- The column is drawn for as long as it is on screen, which is past the
    -- point the menu itself calls closed: a column that vanished the frame it
    -- was dismissed would sink into nothing. The view is the one the menu last
    -- built rather than a fresh one, because a page has nothing to say about a
    -- menu that is shut and the picture sliding away should be the picture
    -- that was there.
    if menu.open then
        self.menu_view = menu.view()
        -- The ship stop opens a panel rather than a page of rows: five parts
        -- of a ship over the credits they are bought with, built here rather
        -- than in the menu because the level it is open at is the interface's
        -- state. The head carries the line that says what closing it will do.
        if menu.stop_open() == "ship" then
            self.menu_view.panel = menu.ship_panel(ui.col_sect, ui.col_hull)
            self.menu_view.foot = ship_foot()
        end
    end
    -- Whether the column is on the screen at all: while it is open and there
    -- is a room for it to be about. Before a room answers the loading screen
    -- is the wordmark on a starfield and nothing else, and everything the
    -- column says is about a room.
    local col_up = menu.open and live
    if self.menu_view and (col_up or ui.column_up()) then
        self.menu_view.open = col_up
        ui.menu(self.menu_view)
    end
    -- The menu's own card, raised over whichever level of the column asked
    -- for it and drawn from one place wherever that is.
    ui.land_card(menu.ask)
    -- The question about moving room, last of everything that publishes a hit
    -- box. `ask_card` drops the ones already published, which is what makes a
    -- card the only thing on screen that can be pressed, and it can only do
    -- that from here: drawn inside the HUD it would clear nothing but its own
    -- panel and leave the menu's rows live underneath.
    ui.room_card(my_rooms())
    -- Not over the menu: the stick and the pads belong to flight, and drawn
    -- under a panel they read as buttons that do nothing. Not while watching
    -- either, for the same reason: there is nothing for them to drive. And
    -- not between matches: the whistle benched every hull, and the ending's
    -- foot keys sit exactly where the pads draw.
    if live and not menu.open and not watching
        and not ui.match_ended(net.match) then
        touch.draw(layers.ui, ww, wh, density)
    end
    session.browser.finish(self, dt, wh, html5, touch, ui, menu, sfx, apply_menu)

    -- Whether anything on screen this frame is frosted, which the render
    -- script has to know before it draws the world: a frame with frost in it
    -- is drawn into a texture so the blur has something to read, and a frame
    -- without it goes straight at the screen as it always has. Read off the
    -- layer rather than kept as a second copy, because the layer already
    -- knows: what the flush counted is exactly what will be drawn.
    --
    -- Posted only when the answer changes, like the viewport message the
    -- render script sends back the other way. Nothing in a fight asks to be
    -- read through, and a fight should not pay for the machinery, down to the
    -- table this would put in the queue every frame.
    local frosted = layers.frost.n > 0
    if frosted ~= self.frosted then
        self.frosted = frosted
        msg.post("@render:", "frost", {on = frosted})
    end

end

-- What the menu asked for.
--
-- The menu names an action and decides nothing; this is the only place that
-- knows what any of them mean, which is what keeps the tree a description of
-- itself rather than a second copy of the game.
-- Into a game: everything a join tears down and dials, shared by the row a
-- player presses and the rejoin the client makes when its identity moves.
function session.enter(self, address, zone_name, wt, room, watch, instance)
    -- A join a press asked for supersedes anything the zone stop asked for.
    self.want_zone = nil
    -- Whatever we were in, we are leaving.
    --
    -- The rooms panel is the one thing left standing. Moving room is the one
    -- join that happens from inside a list, and the list is where you were
    -- looking: shutting it would answer the press by taking away the thing
    -- that asked the question, and you arrive with no idea which of them you
    -- landed in. Its question is dropped by leave_world, since that was about
    -- a press that has now happened.
    self.replay = nil
    self.replay_loading = nil
    self.replay_after = nil
    self.replay_tries = nil
    leave_world()
    directory.close()
    menu.note = "joining " .. zone_name
    -- The one address serves the whole join: the directory named it, and
    -- the zone's name travels with the request so an instance that has
    -- since changed game refuses rather than surprising anybody.
    local started = net.connect(address, menu.class, menu.name, function(why)
        menu.note = why
        go_home(self)
    end, zone_name, watch, wt, room, instance)
    if not started then return false end
    self.online = true
    self.joining = 0
    self.join_progress = net.join_progress
    return true
end

-- Open a public match film without joining an arena. Playback fills the same
-- simulation and public roster tables as a watcher connection, so the normal
-- camera, score and effects draw it without a second game screen.
function session.replay(self, reply)
    leave_world()
    local state, why = session.playback.load(reply, sim, net)
    if not state then
        menu.note = why or "cannot load match replay"
        go_home(self)
        return false
    end
    self.replay = state
    self.online = false
    self.joining = nil
    watching = true
    me = 255
    menu.zone = net.zone
    menu.note = nil
    menu.close()
    return true
end

-- Which game to dial when there is no room on screen.
--
-- A press names one and that one is dialed even while it is down, because a
-- row nobody is serving still wears the dial that says it is being looked for
-- and a press on it is a join that waits. Otherwise it is the game this client
-- was in last, which is what opening the app puts you back into, and the
-- deployment's own front door for a first boot or for a game that has since
-- gone. See `directory.head`.
local function seek_row(self)
    local rows = directory.rows or {}
    if self.want_zone and self.want_zone ~= "" then
        for _, row in ipairs(rows) do
            if row.zone == self.want_zone then return row end
        end
        return nil
    end
    for _, row in ipairs(rows) do
        if row.zone == menu.zone and row.live
           and (row.address or "") ~= "" then
            return row
        end
    end
    return directory.head()
end

-- The client is in a room, or looking for one. This is the loop that puts it
-- there: with no room on screen, dial one as a watcher and let the ordinary
-- arrival do the rest.
--
-- It used to be the attract loop, and what it dialed was scenery: a battle
-- behind the front page, on a connection the deploy press promoted into a
-- real join. That made two of everything. Two ways to be in a room, two
-- meanings for the ship stop, two screens with two sets of instruments, and
-- what it bought was a picture behind a panel. A watcher connection is a
-- session; the pilot in it has no hull yet, which is the same thing a benched
-- pilot has. See decision 156.
--
-- Nothing about a seat is decided here. `menu_go` and the TAKE SEAT chip ask
-- the room for a hull on the connection this already made, so pressing play
-- never dials anything.
function session.seek_tick(self, dt)
    self.seek_wait = math.max((self.seek_wait or 0) - dt, 0)
    -- A film is a world of its own with no arena behind it.
    if self.replay then return end
    -- Already in a room, or on the way into one.
    if self.online then
        -- And where a press asked to be. The press stands down once it has
        -- been served, or every later refresh of the list would drag the
        -- glass back to a choice already made.
        if self.want_zone and menu.zone == self.want_zone then
            self.want_zone = nil
        end
        return
    end
    if self.seek_wait > 0 then return end
    local r = seek_row(self)
    if not r or not r.live or (r.address or "") == "" then
        -- A game a press named that nobody is serving any more. The row was
        -- live when the list was drawn, and the list is not on screen now, so
        -- the loading screen says which game it is waiting for rather than
        -- turning a dial at a starfield.
        if self.want_zone and self.want_zone ~= "" and directory.answered then
            menu.note = "nobody is running "
                .. directory.label_of(self.want_zone)
        end
        return
    end
    -- Grown before the dial, so a zone that refuses instantly cannot be
    -- dialed every frame.
    self.seek_wait = 5
    local started = net.connect(r.address, menu.class, menu.name, function(why)
        menu.note = why
        go_home(self)
    end, r.zone, true, r.wt, nil, r.instance)
    if not started then return end
    self.online = true
    self.joining = 0
    self.join_progress = net.join_progress
    -- The game this client is on, watching or flying: one question with one
    -- answer, so the games list opens on the room already in the glass rather
    -- than on whichever one was last deployed into, and coming back tomorrow
    -- lands where you left off.
    menu.zone = r.zone
    menu.save_identity()
end

-- Take a seat in the room already on screen.
--
-- The one press the column's key is, for anybody watching: a client that has
-- just opened, and a pilot who handed their seat back or was benched by a
-- whistle. The room already holds this connection and owes it only a hull, so
-- this asks where it stands rather than dialing anything. The caps answer, and
-- a room that filled while you watched refuses, which leaves you exactly where
-- you were.
--
-- The seat is asked for first because a room takes a build only from a pilot
-- in one, and the two messages travel in order on the same reliable wire.
--
-- And it is the press that spends a draft from the stands: the hull it asks
-- for is whatever the ship stop names, which is the drafted one where the
-- panel left something pending. Kept before the close, since closing drops a
-- draft nobody spent. See decision 157.
--
-- It used to route through `apply_menu("ship")`, and decision 136 took the
-- watching arm out of that function when the ship stop learned to draft: both
-- callers went on calling it and it had stopped doing anything, so TAKE SEAT
-- and the column's key were controls that made a noise and no difference.
function session.take_seat(self)
    if not (self.online and net.connected) then return false end
    local cls = menu.class
    menu.draft_keep()
    net.set_class(cls)
    menu.send_build(cls)
    -- The board, offered once, to a pilot who now has something to fly with
    -- it. A watcher has no controls to be told about.
    ui.help_prompt = menu.needs_help_prompt()
    menu.close()
    return true
end

-- May this pilot spend a hull, right now?
--
-- Alive and full, or nothing. The rule itself lives in the core so both ends
-- hold exactly one copy of it; this exists only so a refusal arrives with a
-- sentence instead of in silence, and `what` is the half of that sentence
-- which differs between the things it gates. Sets the note and answers false
-- when the answer is no.
local function full_bar(what)
    -- No seat at all is the same answer as a seat that is down, and it is
    -- reachable: a join that has not landed yet leaves this client online
    -- with the menu already up and no ship to ask about.
    if not me or sim.ship_alive(me) ~= 1 then
        menu.note = "not while you are waiting to respawn"
        return false
    end
    if sim.ship_energy(me) < sim.ship_max_energy(me) then
        menu.note = what .. " needs a full bar"
        return false
    end
    return true
end

-- Where the ship this panel is drafting gets spent, said on its head while
-- the panel is still open.
--
-- It names the key, because the key is the one control that spends a draft and
-- it is off screen while the panel stands: the column slid out through the
-- bottom edge to let this up, so a pilot who turned the carousel has nothing
-- in front of them saying what happens next. Back out and the key is there
-- wearing the same words.
--
-- Nothing at all until something has actually been changed: a panel opened to
-- look at your own ship is not an ask, and a line on one that says "you
-- respawn" is a threat about nothing.
--
-- It used to say what *closing* would do, because closing is what settled the
-- draft. Six ways out of a panel all spending a respawn is a menu that acts on
-- a hand waved past it. See decision 157.
function ship_foot()
    if not menu.drafted() then return nil end
    if watching then return "PLAY takes a seat in it" end
    if not me or sim.ship_alive(me) ~= 1 then
        return "not while you are waiting"
    end
    if sim.ship_energy(me) < sim.ship_max_energy(me) then
        return "needs a full bar"
    end
    return "FLY " .. string.upper(menu.hull_name()) .. " respawns you"
end

function apply_menu(self, act)
    if act == "ship" then
        -- The hull this client will arrive in, with no room to ask. In a room
        -- the ship panel drafts and the column's key is what asks, because in
        -- there a ship is the hull and the build together and changing it is
        -- one act rather than one per arrow.
        if not self.online then
            menu.class = menu.pending
            menu.save_identity()
        end
        return
    end

    if act == "fullscreen" then
        if html5 then
            -- Fullscreen brings the keyboard lock along where the browser has
            -- one, and the lock is what lets Ctrl be a gun: windowed, Ctrl
            -- with Tab is the browser's tab switcher on every platform, and
            -- on a Mac the system takes Ctrl with the arrows before the
            -- browser exists. The template clears vwLocked when fullscreen
            -- goes, however it goes, including Chrome's hold-escape.
            pcall(html5.run, "(document.fullscreenElement" ..
                  " ? document.exitFullscreen()" ..
                  " : document.documentElement.requestFullscreen().then(" ..
                  "function(){return navigator.keyboard&&navigator.keyboard.lock" ..
                  "?navigator.keyboard.lock().then(function(){window.vwLocked=1})" ..
                  ":0}).catch(function(){}))")
        end
        return
    end

    if act == "join" then
        local r = menu.chosen
        if not r then return end
        if not r.live then
            -- The row is already wearing a dial that says it is being looked
            -- for, so this only has to not be silent: a press that produces
            -- nothing reads as a dead screen. The zone is remembered so the
            -- note can be taken down when the list stops agreeing with it.
            menu.note = "nobody is running " .. r.name
            note_zone = r.name
            return
        end
        -- Bars off, on the way in.
        --
        -- A phone in a tab gives the game about four fifths of its screen and
        -- puts the browser's own furniture along the edge the thumbs are
        -- already on. Fullscreen takes all of it back and asks the player
        -- nothing, which is worth more than any offer to install could be:
        -- this is the tap that starts a game, and a fullscreen request needs
        -- a tap behind it or the browser refuses it, so there is no other
        -- moment to do it in.
        --
        -- Only where a finger has been. On a desktop the window is the
        -- player's to size and taking the screen because they clicked a game
        -- would be the same rudeness this is meant to undo.
        --
        -- The page holds the rest of the policy: once rather than per join,
        -- and never again once they have put the bars back.
        if html5 and touch.used then
            pcall(html5.run, "window.vwFullOnPlay && window.vwFullOnPlay()")
        end
        menu.zone = r.zone
        menu.save_identity()
        session.enter(self, r.address, r.zone, r.wt, menu.chosen_room,
                      false, r.instance)
        -- Spent. A room is named by the press that named it and by nothing
        -- afterwards: a reconnect the client makes on its own belongs wherever
        -- the ladder puts it, not wherever somebody clicked ten minutes ago.
        menu.chosen_room = nil
        return
    end

    -- A game picked off the zone stop, and what the key does when there is a
    -- seat to hand back: leave whatever this client is in, and put the stands
    -- down on whichever game was named. Picking the game you are in is the
    -- plain leave that had a stop of its own until decision 136, and it lands
    -- in the same place, since the front page is that game watched from the
    -- stands.
    if act == "leave_for" then
        local to = menu.pending
        menu.note = nil
        sfx.ui("ui_go")
        -- The game already on the glass, picked by somebody with no seat in
        -- it. Nothing to leave and nothing to dial: the room this names is the
        -- room being watched, which is what the mark on that row says.
        if not menu.flying() and net.connected and menu.zone == to then
            menu.close()
            return
        end
        go_home(self)
        -- Dialed at once rather than after the failure timer, which nobody's
        -- failure started.
        self.seek_wait = 0
        if type(to) == "string" and to ~= "" then self.want_zone = to end
        return
    end

end

-- The render script reports the pixel space it projects the interface into.
-- On HTML5 that is not always what window.get_size() says, and the interface
-- has to agree with the projection or it draws off the screen.
function on_message(self, message_id, message)
    if message_id == hash("viewport") then
        self.vw, self.vh = message.w, message.h
        self.hw, self.hh = message.hw, message.hh
        self.density = message.density
        -- How much world one screen pixel covers, which is what stops a
        -- stroke from being drawn thinner than the screen can hold one. Taken
        -- from the projection rather than from the zoom and the pixel ratio,
        -- because those two are what the projection is made of and this way
        -- there is only one place that multiplies them. The interface layer
        -- keeps its own, which is one: it is drawn in pixels already.
        if layers and message.w > 0 then
            local px = message.hw * 2 / message.w
            layers.bg.px = px
            layers.bgglow.px = px
            layers.fill.px = px
            layers.glow.px = px
            -- The terrain mesh has this baked into every face it drew, so a
            -- window meshed against the old one is wrong until it is built
            -- again. Forgetting where it was centered is what asks for that.
            if px ~= self.px then
                self.px = px
                static.tx = nil
            end
        end
        if layers and message.hw and message.hw > 0 then
            -- The two per-frame world layers, sized to how much world the
            -- window can see, since most of what they hold is starfield.
            -- Applied here rather than deferred the way the static layers
            -- are: those are only built when the camera leaves its window, so
            -- handing one an empty buffer loses the terrain until it moves,
            -- while these are written from nothing every frame anyway. A
            -- message is dispatched before the update that draws, so the
            -- buffer swapped in here is filled before anything reads it. A
            -- capacity that has not changed is a no-op, and they move in
            -- steps, so dragging a window edge does not allocate per frame.
            --
            -- Only the sky follows the window. What the other two hold is
            -- hulls, bolts and blasts, and how many of those are on screen is
            -- a property of the room rather than of the window, so they are
            -- sized once where they are made.
            local sky_cap, skyglow_cap = world.sky_budget(message.hw,
                                                          message.hh)
            layers.sky:resize(sky_cap)
            layers.skyglow:resize(skyglow_cap)
            -- The window the terrain has to cover, and the room the two
            -- static layers need to hold it. Both follow the view: see
            -- TUNE.STATIC_STEP.
            local need = math.max(message.hw, message.hh) / 16
            local want = math.ceil(need) + TUNE.STATIC_STEP + 1
            if want < TUNE.STATIC_MIN then want = TUNE.STATIC_MIN end
            if want > TUNE.STATIC_MAX then
                -- Said once, because a gap on a window this wide is now a
                -- deliberate ceiling rather than the bug it used to be, and
                -- somebody looking at one deserves to find out why.
                if static_half ~= TUNE.STATIC_MAX then
                    print(string.format(
                        "TERRAIN: view wants %d tiles either side, holding at %d",
                        want, TUNE.STATIC_MAX))
                end
                want = TUNE.STATIC_MAX
            end
            if want ~= static_half then
                static_half = want
                -- The capacities follow from what the next build measures,
                -- not from the window: see fit_static.
                static.tx = nil
            end
        end
    end
end

-- The finger currently dragging the player list, if there is one: which touch
-- it is, where it went down, and what the list was scrolled to then, so the
-- scroll follows the whole gesture rather than accumulating rounding from
-- each frame's step.
local list_drag = nil

-- Whether a press landed on the player list. The panel publishes one box over
-- its whole area for exactly this, and it is the last of its boxes, so the row
-- boxes over it still win a tap.
-- Which list a finger landed on, by name, or nil for neither. Both panels
-- stand in the same slot and only one of them is ever up, but they keep their
-- own scroll positions, so a drag has to say which one it is moving.
-- Whether a finger landed on the page under the tabs, which is the box the
-- menu just drew it in. Its own test rather than a hit box, because a hit box
-- over the whole page would be the first thing under every press and no row
-- would ever answer one.
local function in_page(self, sx, sy)
    if not menu.open then return false end
    local px, py = sx, (self.vh or 0) - sy
    local x, y, w, h = ui.page_span()
    return w > 0 and px >= x and px <= x + w and py >= y and py <= y + h
end

-- Whether a touch went down on the column: its stops, its key, and whatever
-- one of the stops is holding open. A thumb anywhere on it can push it down,
-- which is the gesture a panel that came up out of the bottom edge asks for.
local function in_column(self, sx, sy)
    if not menu.open then return false end
    local px, py = sx, (self.vh or 0) - sy
    for _, r in ipairs(ui.hits) do
        if r.action == "menu_stop" or r.action == "menu_go"
           or r.action == "menu_row" or r.action == "menu_pick"
           or r.action == "menu_back"
        then
            if px >= r.x and px <= r.x + r.w
               and py >= r.y and py <= r.y + r.h then
                return true
            end
        end
    end
    return false
end

local function in_list(self, sx, sy)
    local px, py = sx, (self.vh or 0) - sy
    for _, r in ipairs(ui.hits) do
        if r.action == "scores" or r.action == "rooms_list" then
            if px >= r.x and px <= r.x + r.w
               and py >= r.y and py <= r.y + r.h then
                return r.action
            end
            return nil
        end
    end
    return nil
end

-- What a press on one of the landing's controls does, named by the action
-- its box publishes rather than by which hand made it. A pointer lands on a
-- box and enter lands on whatever the cursor is standing on, and both arrive
-- here: a keyboard that reached the same controls down a path of its own
-- would be a second front end to keep in step with this one.
--
-- Answers whether it recognized the action, so the pointer's own handler can
-- carry on down its list of everything else on the screen.
function menu_act(self, action, value)
    -- A hand has touched the menu, so the whistle no longer owns it.
    --
    -- The sheet the whistle raises is put away again when the next match
    -- opens, which is right for a pilot who read it and left it standing and
    -- wrong the moment they do anything with it: a panel taken away mid-press
    -- is the interface deciding it knows better. One press of anything in here
    -- hands it back, and after that it goes away when they say so.
    if self and self.whistle_raised then self.whistle_raised = false end
    -- The column's one key: into a seat, or out of one.
    --
    -- Which of the three it is comes off whether this client holds a seat and
    -- whether the ship panel left a hull undecided over it. See decisions 143
    -- and 157.
    if action == "menu_go" then
        ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
        ui.col_sect, ui.col_hull = nil, nil
        -- No seat, whether this client has just opened or has been benched by
        -- a whistle: the room already has us and owes us only a hull, so the
        -- press asks for one where it stands. It flies whatever the ship stop
        -- names, so from out here the same press spends a draft and takes a
        -- seat, which is one act and not two.
        if not menu.flying() then
            if session.take_seat(self) then sfx.ui("ui_go")
            else sfx.ui("ui_deny") end
            return true
        end
        -- A seat with a ship drafted over it: the refit, and the only press
        -- that spends a draft in a match.
        --
        -- One act for the whole ship. A pilot walking the carousel from an
        -- Apex to a Lattice and trading a repel on the way has changed ship
        -- once, not seven times, and it costs one respawn rather than seven.
        -- That is what the panel drafts for.
        --
        -- A refusal holds the draft rather than dropping it. The gate is the
        -- core's, mirrored here only so the answer arrives with a sentence,
        -- and the sentence lands on a menu that is still open beside a key
        -- that will work as soon as the bar fills. It used to drop the draft
        -- and say so in the feed, because the panel had already gone by the
        -- time anything was asked.
        if menu.drafted() then
            if not full_bar("a new ship") then
                sfx.ui("ui_deny")
                return true
            end
            local cls = menu.class
            menu.draft_keep()
            -- The build carries the hull, so this one message is the whole
            -- ask. The server owns the roster and nothing here is predicted: a
            -- ship that flickered back would be worse than one that arrives a
            -- frame late.
            menu.send_build(cls)
            -- And the menu goes, because what is behind it has just changed: a
            -- pilot put back at their start with a fresh bar should be looking
            -- at the map rather than at a panel.
            menu.close()
            sfx.ui("ui_go")
            return true
        end
        -- A seat and nothing pending: hand it back and watch the room you were
        -- in from its own gallery. The connection, the map, the roster and the
        -- delayed channel all stay; what changes is that this client has no
        -- hull in the fight. Gated exactly as a hull change is, since it is the
        -- same act of standing a ship down, and refused with the reason rather
        -- than in silence.
        if not full_bar("sitting out") then
            sfx.ui("ui_deny")
            return true
        end
        net.sit_out()
        menu.close()
        sfx.ui("ui_go")
        return true
    end
    -- A press off the column: the glass beside an open panel, or the fight
    -- around the bare stops. It means one level out, which is what the back on
    -- a panel's head means and what a press beside a panel means everywhere
    -- else in this interface. At the bare stops one level out is the column
    -- itself, and at home there is nothing under that, so `menu.close` leaves
    -- it standing.
    if action == "menu_shut" then
        local stop = menu.stack[1]
        if ship_sect_back() or board_card_back() then
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
            return true
        end
        if menu.page_back() then
            ui.page_scroll = 0
            menu_cursor(stop)
        else
            menu.close()
            ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = nil, nil
        end
        sfx.ui("ui_move")
        return true
    end
    -- One of the column's stops. It opens its page, or acts where it has no
    -- page to open.
    if action == "menu_stop" then
        local act, moved = menu.press_stop(value)
        -- A stop that opened a page opens it at the top of its rows rather
        -- than wherever the last one was read to.
        ui.page_scroll = 0
        -- And the ship stop opens on its own menu, at the ship being flown: a
        -- stop opens where the pilot is rather than where they last read to.
        if value == "ship" then
            ui.col_sect, ui.col_hull = nil, nil
            ui.col_scroll = 0
        end
        -- And the players stop opens on the sheet rather than on a card left
        -- open the last time somebody read one.
        if value == "players" then ui.col_pilot = nil end
        -- And standing on the page's own head. Pressing the stop that is
        -- already open shuts it instead, and then the cursor belongs on the
        -- stop, which is what took the page's place on the screen.
        menu_cursor(value)
        if moved then sfx.ui(act and "ui_go" or "ui_move") end
        if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
        return true
    end
    -- A row of an open list: a game off the zone stop, or an account act. One
    -- press, whichever it is, since a list says them all at once and no row is
    -- walked through on the way.
    --
    -- Where an act raises a card the stop stays open under it. That is the
    -- whole of what stacking is: back steps out of the log-in panel onto the
    -- account acts rather than all the way to the column, and the column does
    -- not slide home behind a panel that covers it.
    if action == "menu_pick" then
        local act, moved = menu.press_row(value)
        if moved then sfx.ui("ui_go") end
        if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
        return true
    end
    -- A row of the settings panel. Enter presses it; left and right step it
    -- where it holds a range, which is `menu_step` below.
    if action == "menu_row" then
        local depth = #menu.stack
        local act, moved = menu.press_row(value)
        ui.page_scroll = 0
        -- A row that opened a page of its own is a stop one level in, so the
        -- page it opened stands on its head like any other. A row that only
        -- set something keeps the cursor where the hand left it.
        if #menu.stack > depth then menu_cursor() end
        if moved then sfx.ui(act and "ui_go" or "ui_move") end
        if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
        return true
    end
    if action == "menu_step" then
        if type(value) ~= "table" then return true end
        local act, moved = menu.step_row(value.index, value.dir)
        if moved then sfx.ui("ui_move") end
        if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
        return true
    end
    -- Out of the page a stop opened, one level. At the first level that is
    -- the stop shutting, which leaves the bare column standing.
    if action == "menu_back" then
        local stop = menu.stack[1]
        -- Out of a part of the ship onto the ship menu first, or off a
        -- pilot's card onto the sheet it stands on, which is one level like
        -- any other. See `ship_sect_back` and `board_card_back`.
        if ship_sect_back() or board_card_back() then
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
            return true
        end
        if menu.page_back() then
            ui.page_scroll = 0
            menu_cursor(stop)
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
        end
        return true
    end
    -- The band, which is the other way into the players sheet and the one a
    -- stranger finds: pressing the score opens the room the score is about.
    if action == "players_open" then
        if open_players() then sfx.ui("ui_go") end
        return true
    end
    -- A row of that sheet, which opens the card about that pilot. Pressing
    -- the row whose card is already open steps back onto the sheet, which is
    -- how every other toggle in this interface behaves.
    if action == "board_row" then
        local seat = ui.board_seat_of(value)
        if seat == nil then return true end
        if ui.col_pilot == seat then
            board_card_back()
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
            return true
        end
        ui.col_pilot = seat
        ui.page_scroll = 0
        ui.col_sel, ui.col_sel_value = "menu_back", nil
        sfx.ui("ui_go")
        return true
    end
    -- And the one act the card offers: fly for that pilot's side.
    --
    -- Gated exactly as a hull change is, and for the same reason: a weapon in
    -- flight carries the side that fired it, so a change that took effect in
    -- place would turn incoming fire friendly mid-air. Refused with the reason
    -- rather than in silence.
    --
    -- This gate is the near half of that promise and it was the whole of it
    -- for a while, which made the promise false where it mattered. The bar is
    -- read here and read again by the core when the ask lands, so a round
    -- arriving in between is a refusal this side cannot predict; the room
    -- answers those itself now, and `net.no_team` above is where they are
    -- said. See decision 150.
    --
    -- Asking steps back onto the sheet, because that is where the answer is:
    -- your own row crosses into the first group and their side becomes yours.
    -- A card left standing is a panel whose one key has just gone, since a
    -- pilot on your own side has nothing left to offer, and a reader is left
    -- looking at the one place in the client that cannot show them what they
    -- asked for.
    if action == "board_join" then
        if value == nil then return true end
        if not full_bar("changing sides") then
            sfx.ui("ui_deny")
            return true
        end
        net.set_team(value)
        board_card_back()
        sfx.ui("ui_go")
        return true
    end
    -- The panel's own glass. It swallows a press that missed a row so that one
    -- does not fall through to the backdrop and put the panel away: with the
    -- glass covering most of the window, a thumb landing between two rows
    -- would otherwise dismiss the thing it was aiming at.
    if action == "panel_hold" then return true end
    -- One of the five parts of a ship, opened over the menu that names it.
    -- The tray comes with it, so the purse is on screen wherever a credit is
    -- spent, and back steps out onto the menu again.
    if action == "land_sect" then
        ui.col_sect = value
        -- Body opens on the ship being flown rather than wherever it was last
        -- turned to: a section opens where the pilot is.
        ui.col_hull = value == "body" and menu.panel_home() or nil
        -- Standing on the head of what it opened.
        menu_cursor()
        ui.col_scroll = 0
        sfx.ui("ui_go")
        return true
    end
    -- A flair row: the wake, or which key throws which charge. Enter steps it
    -- forward and the arrows step it either way, which is what a row holding
    -- one of a few answers has always done.
    if action == "land_flair" then
        if menu.flair_step(value, 1) then sfx.ui("ui_go") end
        return true
    end
    -- Turning the carousel: one hull along the roster, wrapping at either
    -- end, and arriving as whatever it lands on.
    --
    -- Turning is the choice. It used to be a look, with a press on the ship
    -- to commit, which is a step a pilot should not have to find: the panel
    -- draws the ship they turned to, names it, and reads out how it flies,
    -- and then flew a different one until they pressed. What changes as the
    -- arrows move is what they will arrive in. See decision 118.
    --
    -- An ask per step is free because of what `apply_menu` does with it. With
    -- no room it remembers the hull and saves it, sending nothing and closing
    -- nothing; in one `menu.pick_profile` writes the draft instead and reports
    -- nothing at all, so the whole turn costs one message, and that one goes
    -- when the key is pressed. See `menu_go`.
    if action == "land_page_ship" then
        local at = menu.hull_page(ui.col_hull or menu.panel_home(), value or 1)
        ui.col_hull = at
        local act = menu.pick_profile(at)
        if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
        sfx.ui("ui_move")
        return true
    end
    if action == "land_flair_step" then
        if type(value) == "table" and menu.flair_step(value.index, value.dir)
        then
            sfx.ui("ui_move")
        end
        return true
    end
    -- Standing on a row that counts is not doing anything to it: the row is
    -- where a hand on a pad stands so that left and right can spend, and enter
    -- on it does nothing rather than guessing which of the two was meant.
    --
    -- A switch is the exception, because there is nothing to guess. It holds
    -- one of two answers and both arrows already mean "the other one", so
    -- enter and space mean it too. Without this the only row in the panel a
    -- keyboard could not work was the only row with a single obvious act.
    if action == "land_kit_row" then
        local at = menu.class or 0
        for _, r in ipairs(menu.sect_rows(at, ui.col_sect) or {}) do
            if r.kind == "slot" and r.slot == value and r.toggle then
                spend(menu.build_step(at, value, r.value > 0 and -1 or 1))
                break
            end
        end
        return true
    end
    -- Spending a credit, or handing one back. The menu holds the build and
    -- tells the arena about it, and answers whether the step happened: an
    -- empty purse and a slot already at its ceiling both come back false, and
    -- both are a thing the pilot tried to do. See `spend`.
    if action == "land_kit_step" then
        local at = menu.class or 0
        if type(value) == "table" then
            spend(menu.build_step(at, value.slot, value.dir))
        end
        return true
    end
    -- And back to the hull's own row, which is the whole of the build
    -- manager: there is nothing to name and nothing to save.
    if action == "land_kit_reset" then
        if menu.build_reset(menu.class or 0) then
            sfx.ui("ui_go")
        end
        return true
    end
    -- Standing on the ship is not doing anything to it: the drawing is where
    -- a hand stands so that left and right can turn the carousel, and turning
    -- is what chooses. Enter on it does nothing rather than repeating a
    -- choice already made, which is the rule every value row here follows.
    if action == "land_pick_ship" then return true end
    return false
end

function on_input(self, action_id, action)
    -- What a keyboard typed, while something is waiting to be typed into.
    -- This is the only text entry in the client and it exists for exactly
    -- one card: the account card's name and password lines. The filtering is
    -- the menu's, one printable ASCII character at a time.
    if action_id == hash("text") and action.text then
        local moved = typed(action.text)
        if moved then sfx.ui("ui_move") end
        return moved
    end

    -- A press is a press on whatever it landed on. The interface publishes its
    -- own hit boxes in the pixel space it drew them in, so this needs no
    -- second copy of the layout.
    --
    -- `screen_x`/`screen_y`, in drawable pixels counting up from the bottom.
    -- Never `x`/`y`: on HTML5 Defold scales those into the resolution
    -- game.project asks for rather than the size the canvas actually is, so
    -- on any window that is not exactly 1280 by 800 every button was tested
    -- against the wrong place, and on a phone by a factor of three.
    local function hit(x, y, finger)
        -- Which box, from ui.pick: the one copy of the rule, containment by
        -- rank with publish order breaking ties, and for a finger the near
        -- miss within the touch floor. See M.pick in ui.lua.
        local r = ui.pick(x, (self.vh or 0) - y, finger)
        if r then
            -- The controls that exist while the menu is shut.
            if r.action == "help_prompt_close" then
                ui.help_prompt = false
                menu.dismiss_help_prompt()
                sfx.ui("ui_move")
                return true
            end
            if r.action == "help_prompt_open" then
                toggle_help()
                sfx.ui("ui_go")
                return true
            end
            if r.action == "open" then
                toggle_menu()
                sfx.ui("ui_go")
                return true
            end
            if r.action == "take_seat" then
                if session.take_seat(self) then sfx.ui("ui_go")
                else sfx.ui("ui_deny") end
                return true
            end
            -- The menu's own controls, which the keyboard reaches through
            -- the same function. See `menu_act`.
            if menu_act(self, r.action, r.value) then return true end

            if r.action == "map" then
                toggle_map()
                sfx.ui("ui_move")
                return true
            end
            -- The rooms list, and the scoreboard it stands in front of.
            -- One panel holds the left column, so opening either puts the
            -- other away rather than drawing both into the same pixels.
            if r.action == "rooms" then
                ui.rooms_open = not ui.rooms_open
                ui.room_scroll = 0
                ui.room_ask = nil
                sfx.ui("ui_move")
                return true
            end
            -- The panel itself, which swallows a press that missed a row
            -- so it cannot fall through to the guns behind it.
            if r.action == "rooms_list" then return true end
            -- A room to move to. The address travels with the row, since
            -- a room on another arena server is another process and the
            -- join is a reconnect; which one it is never reaches the
            -- player.
            -- A room pressed is a room asked about. Moving is a reconnect
            -- and a reconnect is a fresh spawn, so it costs everything a
            -- pilot is carrying; the second press is what pays it.
            -- Pressing the same row again puts the question away, which is
            -- how every other toggle in here behaves.
            if r.action == "room" then
                ui.room_ask = (ui.room_ask ~= r.value) and r.value or nil
                sfx.ui(ui.room_ask and "ui_go" or "ui_move")
                return true
            end
            if r.action == "room_answer" then
                local want = ui.room_ask
                ui.room_ask = nil
                if r.value ~= 1 then
                    sfx.ui("ui_move")
                    return true
                end
                local rm = nil
                for _, r2 in ipairs(my_rooms() or {}) do
                    if r2.n == want then rm = r2 end
                end
                if rm then
                    -- `live` because the join path refuses a row without
                    -- it and says nobody is running the game. That flag
                    -- means "the directory named an address for this",
                    -- which is exactly what a room in this list is: it
                    -- came from the same reply, and a room nobody serves
                    -- is not in it at all.
                    menu.chosen = {name = menu.zone, zone = menu.zone,
                                   address = rm.address, wt = rm.wt,
                                   live = true}
                    -- The room's own number, which is what the server
                    -- answers a join by and what the panel drew. Its
                    -- position in any list is not a handle: reclaiming an
                    -- empty room moves every position after it.
                    menu.chosen_room = rm.n
                    apply_menu(self, "join")
                    sfx.ui("ui_go")
                end
                return true
            end
            -- Ask a pilot to fly with you, which is the one act a private
            -- side has and is still sent from the card a row opens.
            if r.action == "invite" then
                net.invite(r.value)
                sfx.ui("ui_go")
                return true
            end
            if r.action == "debug" then
                ui.debug = not ui.debug
                sfx.ui("ui_move")
                return true
            end
            -- One of the fixed phrases, off the card between matches. The
            -- room refuses it while a match is running, so a press that
            -- lands late is a press that does nothing rather than one the
            -- client has to guard.
            -- Nothing publishes this box any more: the phrase chips went
            -- with the ending's card, and the key meant to replace them is
            -- not here yet. The send stays wired so that key is a binding
            -- rather than a rebuild. See `M.SAY_LIFE` in ui.lua.
            if r.action == "say" then
                net.say(r.value)
                sfx.ui("ui_go")
                return true
            end
            -- The run log swallows the click so a tap that lands on it does
            -- not fall through to whatever is behind it: nothing in it is a
            -- control, and a press through a solid panel would fire into a
            -- wall.
            if r.action == "run_log" then return true end
            -- A card stands with the column shut, and it is the only thing
            -- on screen that can be pressed while it is up: `ask_card` drops
            -- every box published before it. So the handlers below answer for
            -- it too, and the two the card publishes, `answer` and `field`,
            -- are the only ones they can be reached by.
            if not menu.open and not menu.ask then return false end
            local act, moved
            if r.action == "answer" then
                act, moved = menu.click_answer(r.value)
            elseif r.action == "field" then
                moved = menu.focus_field(r.value)
            else
                return false
            end
            if moved then sfx.ui(act and "ui_go" or "ui_move") end
            if act then apply_menu(self, act) end
            return true
        end
        return false
    end

    -- A press of a real mouse button is a hand on the mouse, so a click
    -- brings the drawn cursor back even when it has not travelled, and it
    -- carries a position because a fully faded cursor has forgotten its
    -- own. Before the handlers below, because the ones that consume a
    -- press return.
    if not touch.used and action.pressed
       and (action_id == hash("pointer")
            or action_id == hash("pointer_alt")) then
        self.cursor_x = action.screen_x or action.x
        self.cursor_y = action.screen_y or action.y
        self.cursor_idle = 0
    end

    if action_id == hash("touch") then
        -- What time the stick thinks it is. Only its double tap reads this,
        -- and it is set here rather than beside the rest of touch.lua's fields
        -- in update() because a release can arrive on the line below: a clock
        -- a frame old would be measuring the gap between two taps against the
        -- wrong end of it.
        touch.now = self.clock or 0
        -- Every touch that went down this frame, not just whichever one the
        -- engine decided was primary: on a phone the finger that reaches the
        -- button is often the second one down.
        if action.touch then
            touch.note_used()
            -- UI presses below may consume the whole action. Release every
            -- other finger first so a pad cannot remain held behind that return.
            for _, t in ipairs(action.touch) do
                if t.released then touch.release(t.id) end
            end
        end
        -- The press the mouse action this touch raised has already made, if it
        -- got in first. Taken off `pointer_took` here because this action is
        -- the touch it belongs to, whichever finger turns out to be carrying
        -- it.
        local echo = pointer_took
        pointer_took = nil

        -- Whichever list is up, dragged. It could only ever be scrolled by a
        -- wheel, and a phone has no wheel, so on glass the list simply stopped
        -- at whatever fitted and a room of sixty-four showed nine of them. The
        -- rooms list stands in the same slot and needed the same gesture: nine
        -- rows fit, a zone may hold a hundred, and the rows past the ninth
        -- were reachable by nothing on a phone.
        --
        -- A finger that lands on the list might mean either of two things and
        -- there is no telling which until it moves, so the choice is made on
        -- release: one that travelled was a scroll, one that did not was a tap
        -- on the row it landed on. That is why this runs ahead of the hit
        -- boxes, rather than letting a row open a card under a finger that was
        -- on its way somewhere.
        --
        -- `dragged` is the one finger the list took, so the rest of the frame
        -- leaves it alone and nothing else. The other thumb may be flying, and
        -- swallowing the whole action would stop the stick mid-turn for as
        -- long as somebody was reading the scoreboard.
        local dragged = nil
        if action.touch then
            for _, t in ipairs(action.touch) do
                local sx, sy = t.screen_x or t.x, t.screen_y or t.y
                local which = t.pressed and in_list(self, sx, sy) or nil
                -- A menu page is dragged by the same gesture and settled the
                -- same way, and it is the one the phone actually needed: the
                -- ship page is fourteen hundred points of ladders in eight
                -- hundred of screen. Pixel for pixel rather than rounded to
                -- rows, because a page is not a list of them.
                if not which and t.pressed and in_page(self, sx, sy) then
                    which = "page"
                end
                -- Anywhere else on the column takes the gesture too, so the
                -- stops and the key can push it down. They have nothing to
                -- scroll, so those only ever go downward.
                if not which and t.pressed and in_column(self, sx, sy) then
                    which = "column"
                end
                if which then
                    local rooms = which == "rooms_list"
                    local page = which == "page"
                    list_drag = {id = t.id, x = sx, y = sy, rooms = rooms,
                                 page = page, column = which == "column",
                                 at = page and ui.page_scroll
                                      or ui.room_scroll,
                                 moved = false}
                    dragged = t.id
                elseif list_drag and (list_drag.page or list_drag.column)
                       and t.id == list_drag.id then
                    dragged = t.id
                    -- screen_y counts up from the bottom, so a finger moving
                    -- up the screen raises it and the page should follow it,
                    -- which is the opposite sign from a list of rows.
                    local dy = sy - list_drag.y
                    local dx = sx - list_drag.x
                    -- Which way this gesture is going, decided once and then
                    -- kept. A drag that changed its mind halfway would scroll
                    -- the page and push the drawer in the same stroke, and a
                    -- thumb travelling down a list is never perfectly vertical.
                    if math.abs(dx) > 10 or math.abs(dy) > 10 then
                        list_drag.moved = true
                    end
                    if not list_drag.axis then
                        -- A page scrolls under a thumb; the column itself has
                        -- nothing to scroll, so a drag that starts on it is a
                        -- drag pushing it back down. Decided once and kept: a
                        -- thumb travelling down a list is never perfectly
                        -- vertical, and a gesture that changed its mind
                        -- halfway would scroll the page and shut the column in
                        -- one stroke.
                        if list_drag.page and math.abs(dy) > 10 then
                            list_drag.axis = "up"
                        elseif dy < -10 and math.abs(dy) > math.abs(dx) then
                            list_drag.axis = "down"
                        end
                    end
                    if list_drag.axis == "up" then
                        ui.page_scroll = list_drag.at + dy
                    end
                    if t.released then
                        if list_drag.axis == "down" then
                            -- Far enough to mean it. Less than that is a thumb
                            -- that thought better of it, and the column stays
                            -- where it is rather than being left ajar.
                            if -dy > 40 then
                                menu.close()
                                -- The same bleep every other press that moves
                                -- the cursor makes. There are two UI sounds in
                                -- this game, `ui_move` and `ui_go`; putting a
                                -- panel away is not an act on anything.
                                sfx.ui("ui_move")
                            end
                        elseif not list_drag.moved then
                            hit(sx, sy, true)
                        end
                        list_drag = nil
                    end
                elseif list_drag and t.id == list_drag.id then
                    dragged = t.id
                    -- screen_y counts up from the bottom, so a finger moving
                    -- up the screen raises it, and dragging up should bring
                    -- the rows below into view.
                    --
                    -- Measured from where the finger went down rather than
                    -- from the last frame, so the rounding to whole rows
                    -- happens once over the gesture instead of accumulating
                    -- a lost pixel per frame.
                    local dy = sy - list_drag.y
                    local pitch = math.max(1, ui.row_pitch())
                    -- Better than half a row before it counts as travel,
                    -- which is the width of the wobble in a tap.
                    if math.abs(dy) > pitch * 0.6 then list_drag.moved = true end
                    -- Rounded toward zero, so the same small wobble counts the
                    -- same in both directions. math.floor runs toward negative
                    -- infinity, which made one pixel of downward drift a whole
                    -- row of scroll while a pixel upward was none: a tap that
                    -- shifted the list under its own finger and opened the row
                    -- that slid into the gap.
                    local rows = dy / pitch
                    rows = (rows < 0) and math.ceil(rows) or math.floor(rows)
                    local to = list_drag.at + rows
                    ui.room_scroll = to
                    if t.released then
                        if not list_drag.moved then hit(sx, sy, true) end
                        list_drag = nil
                    end
                end
            end
        end

        -- Which fingers the interface took, rather than whether any did.
        --
        -- A press landing on a hit box used to return from here, and Defold
        -- delivers every touch point of a frame in one action: the other
        -- thumb's press, move and release went with it. Lifting off the guns
        -- pad in the same frame as a tap on a chip meant the release was never
        -- seen, the pad stayed latched, and the ship fired with no finger down
        -- until some later touch happened to reuse the id -- which on the web,
        -- where ids are not reused, is never.
        local claimed = dragged and {[dragged] = true} or nil
        if action.touch then
            for _, t in ipairs(action.touch) do
                if t.pressed and not (claimed and claimed[t.id]) then
                    local tx, ty = t.screen_x or t.x, t.screen_y or t.y
                    -- One tap is one press. Where the echo has already
                    -- pressed the control under this finger there is nothing
                    -- left for the finger to do but say it was taken, so the
                    -- stick and the pads leave it alone. One finger's worth
                    -- of it: a second going down in the same frame is a press
                    -- of its own.
                    local took = echo
                        and echo == ui.pick(tx, (self.vh or 0) - ty, true)
                    if took then echo = nil end
                    if took or hit(tx, ty, true) then
                        claimed = claimed or {}
                        claimed[t.id] = true
                    end
                end
            end
        end
        -- Watching on glass: the stick and the pads have nothing to drive and
        -- the camera is the room's, so a finger on the arena does nothing.
        if net.watching then return end
        touch.on_touch(action, self.vw or 0, self.vh or 0, self.density or 1,
                       claimed)
        return
    end
    -- A click faster than a frame never reaches this line at all. See the note
    -- on the latch at the end of this function: it is the same fact for the
    -- mouse as for the keyboard, and it is the engine's doing rather than ours.
    if action_id == hash("pointer") and action.pressed and not touch.used then
        local px = action.screen_x or action.x
        local py = action.screen_y or action.y
        -- Which control the press is on, asked before it is made: a touch may
        -- have raised this mouse action, and that touch is handed over next.
        -- See `pointer_took`.
        local on = ui.pick(px, (self.vh or 0) - py, false)
        if hit(px, py) then
            pointer_took = on
            return
        end
    end

    -- The wheel, which belongs to whichever list is on screen: the menu when
    -- it is open, and under it whichever panel holds the left column, since
    -- the rooms list and the scoreboard stand in the same slot and only one of
    -- them is ever up. Defold reports a wheel notch as a press and a release
    -- of the same action, so only the press counts or every notch would move
    -- two.
    if action_id == hash("wheel_up") or action_id == hash("wheel_down") then
        if action.pressed then
            local d = (action_id == hash("wheel_up")) and -1 or 1
            if menu.open then
                -- The page under the tabs. A notch is a couple of rows rather
                -- than one, because a menu page is read by the screenful and
                -- a list is read by the row.
                ui.page_scroll = ui.page_scroll + d * ui.row_pitch() * 2.5
                land_slid()
            elseif ui.rooms_open then
                ui.room_scroll = ui.room_scroll + d
            end
        end
        return
    end

    if action_id == nil then
        -- The mouse, moving. A nil action can arrive without any travel in
        -- it, so movement is the position having changed, not the event
        -- having landed. The position is kept in drawable pixels, the same
        -- space the hit boxes are tested in; the cursor block in update
        -- turns it into a drawn pointer and fades it once the hand is still.
        local mx = action.screen_x or action.x
        local my = action.screen_y or action.y
        if mx ~= self.cursor_x or my ~= self.cursor_y then
            self.cursor_x, self.cursor_y = mx, my
            self.cursor_idle = 0
        end
        return
    end
    -- The latch. `held` is the key's live state and `tapped` is a press that
    -- has not been read yet, cleared at the end of the update that reads it
    -- rather than on release, so a press and a release inside one frame still
    -- reaches the frame loop.
    --
    -- What it cannot save is a press the engine never reported. Input is polled
    -- once a frame and pressed and released are the differences between two
    -- polls, so a down and an up inside one poll produce no difference and no
    -- action at all: nothing arrives here to be latched. That is true of the
    -- mouse buttons and of every key alike, and it is Defold's input layer
    -- rather than anything in this file.
    --
    -- At 60 Hz it takes a sub-16 ms press, which a hand cannot produce and an
    -- automated driver produces every time. Playwright's click() and press()
    -- are both instantaneous, and under a software renderer the frames are long
    -- enough to swallow them whole. A script driving this has to hold for
    -- ~90 ms or it will conclude the interface is dead when it is not. Two
    -- sessions have now reached exactly that conclusion, one about the mouse
    -- and one about M and Esc, which is why the note is here rather than in the
    -- pointer branch where only half of it was visible.
    --
    -- A key arrives as itself, since the binding file is fixed at build time
    -- and what a key does is not. It is latched twice: under the key, which is
    -- what the menu's arrows and anything else about the keyboard rather than
    -- the ship read, and under whatever control is on it, which is what the
    -- rest of this file reads and the reason none of it knows a binding
    -- happened. A key nothing is bound to latches once and is simply never
    -- asked about.
    --
    -- Which control that is depends on what else is down, since a binding may
    -- be a chord: Shift and Tab together may be one control where Tab alone is
    -- another. The question is asked on the press and the answer is
    -- remembered, so a key goes on holding whatever it started until it comes
    -- up. Deciding again on the release would let a modifier lifted early hand
    -- the press to the barer binding halfway through, which is a bomb thrown
    -- by letting go of shift.
    local name = action_id
    if action.pressed then
        held[name] = true
        tapped[name] = true
        local did = binds.on_press(name, held)
        if did then
            owner[name] = did
            held[did] = true
            tapped[did] = true
        end
    end
    if action.released then
        held[name] = nil
        lifted[name] = true
        local did = owner[name]
        if did then
            held[did] = nil
            owner[name] = nil
        end
    end
end
